select id, episode_id, cms_item_id, chunk_index, content, token_estimate, speakers, start_timestamp, end_timestamp, podcast_index, episode_name, slug, web_url, guest_name, guest_title, categories, short_description, podcast_length
from jsonb_to_recordset($jsonpayload$
[{"id": "bc6433a1-e02f-4b1b-837a-f2e414919ef7", "episode_id": "5496349b-70a7-4e0f-9581-7ca8e78b24ee", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4826", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Stephen Matthews (01:58): Yeah, yeah, absolutely. So like I said, started in 1981, always Vancouver based throughout the eighties. In the nineties we had a big push in cross country racing. Mountain biking at that time was really emerging as a race sport. We had people attend the Olympics, some of 'em I still work with today. And then in the early two thousands, a movement called Free Ride Mountain biking kicked off and we were really there at the head of it. And so free ride mountain biking truly did start here in North Vancouver. And a couple of the athletes that were pretty instrumental in kicking that off. We latched onto at an early age and they still ride for us today. So guys like Wade Simmons and Thomas Vanderham are still very involved with our brand and really helped give us an identity in the early two thousands as mountain biking moved away from racing into being a bit more of a creative and fun sport.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:53): Yeah, that's awesome. Early on when you guys were looking at partnering with Wade as an athlete, he was kind of early in his career, he was just jumping into this free ride thing that was fairly new. A whole lot of people hadn't heard about it, but you guys, Rocky Mountain, saw something in him and saw what he was doing, and you guys were really intrigued by that. How come? Why?\n\nStephen Matthews (03:16): Yeah. I think it's from the beginning, our beginnings with the person who started Rocky Mountain, for him, it was all about doing something different. So I think when Wade came along, he was kind of like I mentioned, at the forefront of doing something different. And so it was a natural kind of synergy work together. The brand saw a little bit of themselves in this guy who is doing his own thing, and it just happened to work out in a really, really positive way.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:46): And from that free Ride is built kind of from this fun idea being creative. How has that stayed with your brand as you guys have continued to evolve and grow?", "token_estimate": 503, "speakers": ["Stephen Matthews", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "01:58", "end_timestamp": "03:46", "podcast_index": 6, "episode_name": "Ep 6: Stephen Matthews | Race Results Aren't the Only Thing that Matters| Rocky Mountain Bicycles", "slug": "ep-6-stephen-matthews-race-results-arent-the-only-thing-that-matters-rocky-mountain-bicycles", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-6-stephen-matthews-race-results-arent-the-only-thing-that-matters-rocky-mountain-bicycles", "guest_name": "Stephen Matthews", "guest_title": "Brand Manager, Rocky Mountain Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, we chat about the sport of mountain biking, the birth of freeride, and why race results aren't the only thing that matters to a brand like Rocky Mountain.", "podcast_length": "19:08"}, {"id": "ddc51a5a-14d5-460b-afcf-13f162859425", "episode_id": "5496349b-70a7-4e0f-9581-7ca8e78b24ee", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4826", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Stephen Matthews (03:57): Yeah, I think we've taken the idea of doing something that feels like it should be our own and that we should be able to leave our own mark on it. So in Free ride, it's kind of like big mountain skiing versus race skiing, people interpreting, whether it's chute lines or cliffs or a powder field, they're kind of finding their own way down the mountain, and that's what free ride's all about. And so for us, we've taken latching on a free ride and that idea of putting our Rocky Mountain stamp on whatever we do, even if it's back into the race atmosphere. So we have a very successful Enduro race program right now, which is very structured and all that. But we've latched onto the idea of having our own identity and we partner with great athletes. We let them be themselves, and there's an emphasis on them trying to perform, but we also want their personalities to come through. I think mountain biking is something I care a lot about and it's a ton of fun. And I think no matter what the discipline or what side of it, it's about bringing forward that idea of enjoying yourself and having fun (05:10): In the sport.\n\nCole Heilborn (05:11): And why is that something you guys have carried into your pro team? That idea?\n\nStephen Matthews (05:16): I think for me at least from the marketing side and being in charge of the look and feel of the brand, things like race results are only important if you care about race results. And the reality is the majority of people rather get to know a person or what this racer is beyond the race atmosphere. So I definitely really encourage the personalities to shine through because on an individual level and as a team, they're all really interesting people. They're funny, they have their own passions and interests, and so market, the race side of things that keep us at and get us those podiums. And that's still really important to the process, but how do you make it appeal to a wider audience is by giving it more personality.\n\nCole Heilborn (06:04): Yeah. So let's switch gears a little bit. What is your role as brand manager?", "token_estimate": 526, "speakers": ["Stephen Matthews", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "03:57", "end_timestamp": "06:04", "podcast_index": 6, "episode_name": "Ep 6: Stephen Matthews | Race Results Aren't the Only Thing that Matters| Rocky Mountain Bicycles", "slug": "ep-6-stephen-matthews-race-results-arent-the-only-thing-that-matters-rocky-mountain-bicycles", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-6-stephen-matthews-race-results-arent-the-only-thing-that-matters-rocky-mountain-bicycles", "guest_name": "Stephen Matthews", "guest_title": "Brand Manager, Rocky Mountain Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, we chat about the sport of mountain biking, the birth of freeride, and why race results aren't the only thing that matters to a brand like Rocky Mountain.", "podcast_length": "19:08"}, {"id": "02c2d053-6dd4-4263-b0dc-38b499faf534", "episode_id": "5496349b-70a7-4e0f-9581-7ca8e78b24ee", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4826", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Stephen Matthews (06:10): Yeah, it feels like a little bit of everything some days, but I look after all content production for Rocky Mountain General look and feel of the brand. So if there's an ad that comes out or a video that's been made or our race team that's appearing all over the world, I will have had a handle in creating the look and feel of everything that a consumer would see from Rocky Mountain. Yeah, it's a general, I oversee the marketing side of things to make sure that Rocky Mountain is staying true to Rocky Mountain.\n\nCole Heilborn (06:49): And so you guys have done a really good job of putting out video series and projects, speaking to the doing authentic, trying to get away from the results and be more focused on the personalities. You guys have recently been doing a series called the JK series.\n\nStephen Matthews (07:08): Yeah, it's the JK files,\n\nCole Heilborn (07:10): The J files. Excuse me. So tell us a little bit about that, because I think that's something that's really interesting. In the sports world. A lot of people are often focused on the results, but you guys are making a series about just the people who are on the", "token_estimate": 291, "speakers": ["Stephen Matthews", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "06:10", "end_timestamp": "07:10", "podcast_index": 6, "episode_name": "Ep 6: Stephen Matthews | Race Results Aren't the Only Thing that Matters| Rocky Mountain Bicycles", "slug": "ep-6-stephen-matthews-race-results-arent-the-only-thing-that-matters-rocky-mountain-bicycles", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-6-stephen-matthews-race-results-arent-the-only-thing-that-matters-rocky-mountain-bicycles", "guest_name": "Stephen Matthews", "guest_title": "Brand Manager, Rocky Mountain Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, we chat about the sport of mountain biking, the birth of freeride, and why race results aren't the only thing that matters to a brand like Rocky Mountain.", "podcast_length": "19:08"}, {"id": "0faf0854-c079-4dc6-be45-073242b4601b", "episode_id": "5496349b-70a7-4e0f-9581-7ca8e78b24ee", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4826", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Stephen Matthews (07:23): Bikes. It actually kicked off last year, and it was a bit of a rocky start. It was something that myself and the team really wanted to do, but classic way, we hadn't budgeted for it. And so it was a bit of a leap of faith. So when we kicked off the project, I actually got on a plane to the first race, which is in New Zealand, not knowing where the money was going to come to film it, but it ended up working out. And I think it is because of what I had mentioned earlier about this series. The whole premise behind it is these athletes are traveling the world at a very elite level doing things on their bikes that hardly anyone can relate to. And that's just it. Racing is only important if you care about racing and if you don't, it's kind of a lost concept to you. So these people are doing something that's pretty unattainable, and so how do we make them more relatable as characters? And they're all fantastic people individually, they're funny, they all have their quirks. Some are more confident than others, and it's all these personalities that were shining but not being shown. (08:33): And so we created this video series that through the nature of them being at a race, there's definitely a lot of footage of them racing their bikes and all that. But the real nuggets within the series are when they're just being themselves and having fun and joking around, and it's the times outside of the race atmosphere where you can see how they relate to one another that becomes more relatable for people like you and me.\n\nCole Heilborn (08:58): And what was the result from that campaign or the project?\n\nStephen Matthews (09:01): Yeah, really positive, to be honest with you. Like the Enduro World series, which is the race series that they're a part of, do an awesome job of covering results and showing who won what stage and what the overalls are at. And they do a great job, but a part that was missing was to show the personalities of the racers. And so I think all those people that weren't necessarily moved or motivated by seeing the names on a spreadsheet of results were able to connect with the sport in a different way.", "token_estimate": 542, "speakers": ["Stephen Matthews", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "07:23", "end_timestamp": "09:01", "podcast_index": 6, "episode_name": "Ep 6: Stephen Matthews | Race Results Aren't the Only Thing that Matters| Rocky Mountain Bicycles", "slug": "ep-6-stephen-matthews-race-results-arent-the-only-thing-that-matters-rocky-mountain-bicycles", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-6-stephen-matthews-race-results-arent-the-only-thing-that-matters-rocky-mountain-bicycles", "guest_name": "Stephen Matthews", "guest_title": "Brand Manager, Rocky Mountain Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, we chat about the sport of mountain biking, the birth of freeride, and why race results aren't the only thing that matters to a brand like Rocky Mountain.", "podcast_length": "19:08"}, {"id": "35c7c2b3-342c-4b2c-a1c1-c34c1e8169b8", "episode_id": "5496349b-70a7-4e0f-9581-7ca8e78b24ee", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4826", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Cole Heilborn (09:34): So when you guys are looking ahead to the future, how do you continue what you guys have built in the 2021 and beyond?\n\nStephen Matthews (09:43): Yeah. Well, for me, I got involved with mountain biking purely for the passion of it. I loved it as a kid. I was a mountain biker and super motivated to just have fun more than anything. And I'm really happy to say that it segued into a job, eventually took a while, but it did. You\n\nCole Heilborn (10:05): Made it.\n\nStephen Matthews (10:05): I made it, yeah. And so I think it's about maintaining that excitement and the passion for mountain biking and ideas will come. Right now we have a great race team and we're really pushing it. And so how do I take what I love about mountain biking and what Rocky Mountain stands for and bring it to a broader audience through that avenue? And if that change to a different style or a different focus for our marketing, it's really about bringing those things that we as cyclists love forward and making sure they're relatable and feel inclusive for people of any ability, whether it's your first ride or you're an expert. It's about seeing what it is, seeing what you love about mountain biking in our brand.\n\nCole Heilborn (10:50): Yeah. What challenges are you guys currently working through in the marketing department, in the bike industry right now?", "token_estimate": 342, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Stephen Matthews"], "start_timestamp": "09:34", "end_timestamp": "10:50", "podcast_index": 6, "episode_name": "Ep 6: Stephen Matthews | Race Results Aren't the Only Thing that Matters| Rocky Mountain Bicycles", "slug": "ep-6-stephen-matthews-race-results-arent-the-only-thing-that-matters-rocky-mountain-bicycles", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-6-stephen-matthews-race-results-arent-the-only-thing-that-matters-rocky-mountain-bicycles", "guest_name": "Stephen Matthews", "guest_title": "Brand Manager, Rocky Mountain Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, we chat about the sport of mountain biking, the birth of freeride, and why race results aren't the only thing that matters to a brand like Rocky Mountain.", "podcast_length": "19:08"}, {"id": "a47f513e-41fb-42b7-b76a-a09a5b6c8449", "episode_id": "5496349b-70a7-4e0f-9581-7ca8e78b24ee", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4826", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Stephen Matthews (11:00): There's, I think a big challenge for all mountain biking right now is gaining clarity on e-bikes. We've introduced a lineup and it's been incredibly successful for us on a global scale, but it's really touch and go depending on where you are in the world. The US for instance, seems to have spots opening that are open to riding e-bikes and other trail networks that are closed, and it just feels really touch and go right now. So it is a new product and it's a really great product for introducing new riders to the sport. But there's definitely a bit of red tape when it comes to trail usage that just makes it, I think, difficult for a consumer to buy with confidence. And that's just not just our brand, but any brand people looking at at a point right now where there are networks that are just not to be ridden.\n\nCole Heilborn (11:54): So\n\nStephen Matthews (11:55): I think it's a challenge. Yeah, it's a challenge to market that globally when globally they're not accepted everywhere.\n\nCole Heilborn (12:03): Yeah, they've certainly blown up in the last maybe in the six months, they've just kind of taken off. Some of the big brands have started to release them, and now locally, a bunch of people are asking their local trail groups, can we ride these here? And sometimes they're not even sure.\n\nStephen Matthews (12:20): Yeah, I think that's exactly it is. There's a lot of unsure, unsure ideas in the legislation and landowners and land managers because they are a relatively new thing. And as we know, bureaucratic process for getting new activities approved in certain land areas is slow. And so it's a bit of a cart before the horse scenario perhaps.\n\nCole Heilborn (12:48): How did you get to here? You got kind of an interesting story. Can you walk us through that?", "token_estimate": 452, "speakers": ["Stephen Matthews", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "11:00", "end_timestamp": "12:48", "podcast_index": 6, "episode_name": "Ep 6: Stephen Matthews | Race Results Aren't the Only Thing that Matters| Rocky Mountain Bicycles", "slug": "ep-6-stephen-matthews-race-results-arent-the-only-thing-that-matters-rocky-mountain-bicycles", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-6-stephen-matthews-race-results-arent-the-only-thing-that-matters-rocky-mountain-bicycles", "guest_name": "Stephen Matthews", "guest_title": "Brand Manager, Rocky Mountain Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, we chat about the sport of mountain biking, the birth of freeride, and why race results aren't the only thing that matters to a brand like Rocky Mountain.", "podcast_length": "19:08"}, {"id": "36a2d5e5-58f3-4592-bfa8-914ecb1ee42c", "episode_id": "5496349b-70a7-4e0f-9581-7ca8e78b24ee", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4826", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Stephen Matthews (12:52): Yeah. I grew up in Calgary, Alberta, and I have an older brother who's super involved with mountain biking, and we both kind of got into it in our early teens, like I mentioned earlier, just for fun. We loved hitting jumps and riding all over the place, and it was really well timed with the free ride movement that Rocky Mountain latched onto. So when I was a kid, I wasn't interested in racing. I was super interested in riding off jumps and cliffs and log rides, and the creativity that was essentially formed in North Vancouver here was what was inspiring me back in Calgary. And so yeah, I wrote a ton throughout my teens and I got involved with sponsorships and a couple brands that helped get me going. And then from there, I just made a whole bunch of decisions that mountain biking was going to be a part of my life. So I moved to Whistler. I became a guide in Europe for a while, always just taking opportunities. If there was a chance to do a photo shoot or a video shoot being a part of it. They definitely didn't all work out financially for the most part, but it was a lot of fun. And I think for me, looking back, it was just like I took opportunities that there made the best of 'em, and I met some great people and eventually those people helped me get a job and that's where I'm today.\n\nCole Heilborn (14:14): One of the things I noticed and you've said is that you're really passionate about the bike industry. Is there ever a point though, in your job where the passion, it doesn't run out, but that fire kind of slows down for you? Is that a thing?", "token_estimate": 399, "speakers": ["Stephen Matthews", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "12:52", "end_timestamp": "14:14", "podcast_index": 6, "episode_name": "Ep 6: Stephen Matthews | Race Results Aren't the Only Thing that Matters| Rocky Mountain Bicycles", "slug": "ep-6-stephen-matthews-race-results-arent-the-only-thing-that-matters-rocky-mountain-bicycles", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-6-stephen-matthews-race-results-arent-the-only-thing-that-matters-rocky-mountain-bicycles", "guest_name": "Stephen Matthews", "guest_title": "Brand Manager, Rocky Mountain Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, we chat about the sport of mountain biking, the birth of freeride, and why race results aren't the only thing that matters to a brand like Rocky Mountain.", "podcast_length": "19:08"}, {"id": "d37cc415-b19e-42e0-99fc-8f4f9ab03012", "episode_id": "5496349b-70a7-4e0f-9581-7ca8e78b24ee", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4826", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Stephen Matthews (14:28): Not so far. I'm young. I don't know. No, I think for me, honestly, it's really important that I ride, I ride my bike a lot still, and I take opportunities when I can. Even during the workday, we're really lucky being close to our trails, and if I have an opportunity to go for a lunch ride, I'll take it. And if I don't and I have to work through the day, that's fine, I'll go after work. But I think for me, I ride most days because I really enjoy it, and it helps remind me when I'm at work of why I work here. So it hasn't happened yet, but I definitely put a huge concerted effort into make sure that I'm on the bike a lot. Yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (15:12): What's your hope for the bike industry in the future?\n\nStephen Matthews (15:17): I think it's an interesting time. Mountain biking that I fell in love with back when I was 12, 13 years old. It's changed a lot. The sports blown up like crazy. More people than ever are getting involved with it, and I'd like to just see that continue. Really. When I started mountain biking almost, it wasn't underground, but it definitely wasn't mainstream. I've also moved from the prairies of Alberta to the epicenter of mountain biking. So everywhere I go, it is not uncommon to see people on super high-end carbon bikes. But that was definitely not the case when I was growing up. So my hope for the mountain bike industry is just that more people get involved with it, that more people get involved with trail work and help build up those community sides of things. I think we're still in the grand scheme of things, if you compare it to an industry like skiing, we're really young. We're super young, and so we've had a boom, I think we're having another one here with e mountain bike. And in general, I think I'd like to see more people involved with the community side of it and then see where it goes from there. Because reality is, the more people involved with it, the more it's going to move it forward as a\n\nCole Heilborn (16:28): Whole. So if you couldn't have the job that you have now, what would be a dream job?\n\nStephen Matthews (16:38): Sometimes I feel like just working at a coffee shop, to be honest.", "token_estimate": 548, "speakers": ["Stephen Matthews", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "14:28", "end_timestamp": "16:38", "podcast_index": 6, "episode_name": "Ep 6: Stephen Matthews | Race Results Aren't the Only Thing that Matters| Rocky Mountain Bicycles", "slug": "ep-6-stephen-matthews-race-results-arent-the-only-thing-that-matters-rocky-mountain-bicycles", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-6-stephen-matthews-race-results-arent-the-only-thing-that-matters-rocky-mountain-bicycles", "guest_name": "Stephen Matthews", "guest_title": "Brand Manager, Rocky Mountain Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, we chat about the sport of mountain biking, the birth of freeride, and why race results aren't the only thing that matters to a brand like Rocky Mountain.", "podcast_length": "19:08"}, {"id": "81a2ba1c-8e12-4e9c-a774-d7c44036495c", "episode_id": "5496349b-70a7-4e0f-9581-7ca8e78b24ee", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4826", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Cole Heilborn (16:41): Yeah,\n\nStephen Matthews (16:42): No, I don't know. I think if I didn't have the job I did now. I mean, I definitely love riding and I spent many years just riding, so it'd be cool to go back to that. But I don't know. I actually really like my favorite part of my job is the content creation sides of things. So if I was to focus in on say, more of a production or art director job of controlling or helping tell brand stories, I guess that's kind of what I have now. I'm a brand manager.\n\nCole Heilborn (17:15): I think you just\n\nStephen Matthews (17:17): Described my job.\n\nCole Heilborn (17:18): I think you did.\n\nStephen Matthews (17:20): Yeah. I honestly don't know. I really like what I do.\n\nCole Heilborn (17:24): Yeah.\n\nStephen Matthews (17:24): Yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (17:26): So last question.\n\nStephen Matthews (17:28): Yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (17:29): If you could tell someone could tell your 12 or 13-year-old self any advice that you've learned along the way, what would it be?\n\nStephen Matthews (17:40): I would say to follow, I mean, it sounds cliche, but I'd say follow your passion. And if something's important to you, make sure you do. It is a big part of it. The money will come eventually, hopefully. But the biggest thing is that by following what you like to do, you're going to have a great life. And it might not always seem like you're heading in a straight direction. You'll take a couple steps one way or the other. But yeah, I've done lots of photo shoots and video shoots that seemed completely irrelevant, and then the person that I worked for that particular shoot comes back around with an opportunity that's more in line with where I want to go. So it's your classic meet people take opportunities, but I think doing what you truly love is the best way to go through life.\n\nCole Heilborn (18:31): Yeah. Well said. Well, if people wanted to find you, what trail could they find you on around here?\n\nStephen Matthews (18:37): I live just off Mount Seymour, so I spend a lot of time over there. I'll probably be at the bottom drinking coffees\n\nCole Heilborn (18:45): Or making them, if you get", "token_estimate": 536, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Stephen Matthews"], "start_timestamp": "16:41", "end_timestamp": "18:45", "podcast_index": 6, "episode_name": "Ep 6: Stephen Matthews | Race Results Aren't the Only Thing that Matters| Rocky Mountain Bicycles", "slug": "ep-6-stephen-matthews-race-results-arent-the-only-thing-that-matters-rocky-mountain-bicycles", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-6-stephen-matthews-race-results-arent-the-only-thing-that-matters-rocky-mountain-bicycles", "guest_name": "Stephen Matthews", "guest_title": "Brand Manager, Rocky Mountain Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, we chat about the sport of mountain biking, the birth of freeride, and why race results aren't the only thing that matters to a brand like Rocky Mountain.", "podcast_length": "19:08"}, {"id": "b61412d7-56b2-4bcd-8a79-62028bb4400e", "episode_id": "5496349b-70a7-4e0f-9581-7ca8e78b24ee", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4826", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Stephen Matthews (18:46): That or making them, if I get that job, you never know. But yeah, what's a great deal? You know what? Forever After is a trail on Mount Seymour and Rocky Mountain has adopted it to maintain it. And so I do quite a few laps on that one. Awesome. Just because fun to ride your own work.\n\nCole Heilborn (19:02): Yeah. Awesome. Well, thank you for the time today. Thank you. I'm afraid we're running the clock out, but thanks again for sitting down and chatting.", "token_estimate": 121, "speakers": ["Stephen Matthews", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "18:46", "end_timestamp": "19:02", "podcast_index": 6, "episode_name": "Ep 6: Stephen Matthews | Race Results Aren't the Only Thing that Matters| Rocky Mountain Bicycles", "slug": "ep-6-stephen-matthews-race-results-arent-the-only-thing-that-matters-rocky-mountain-bicycles", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-6-stephen-matthews-race-results-arent-the-only-thing-that-matters-rocky-mountain-bicycles", "guest_name": "Stephen Matthews", "guest_title": "Brand Manager, Rocky Mountain Bicycles", "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, we chat about the sport of mountain biking, the birth of freeride, and why race results aren't the only thing that matters to a brand like Rocky Mountain.", "podcast_length": "19:08"}, {"id": "2d6908a7-1183-4560-af65-7c0f718ef23c", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Rick Saez (00:02): Your voice is your most important superpower. I believe the human voice is unique in that you can tell inflection, you can tell mood, you can tell all kinds of things just from hearing somebody speak. So I think it's a pretty powerful medium. And the second thing is, as we saw a couple weeks ago when Facebook crashed, your followers can't always read your blog post or watch your video. But as you're out there on your mountain bike ride, you can stick an earbud in your ear and listen to podcasts all day long that you've downloaded the day before. So I think for an active audience, podcast for a fantastic medium.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:33): Welcome to the Back Country Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hile, born and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam, or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Back Country Marketing Podcast Podcast. This episode is produced by Portside Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. Today I'm sitting down with Rick Ez. He's the founder, host, and producer of the Outdoor Biz Podcast. Rick, how are you doing?\n\nRick Saez (01:15): I'm doing great. How you doing?\n\nCole Heilborn (01:16): Fantastic. I'm super excited to have you on the show today. You're in the podcast studio. This is a pretty normal setup for you.\n\nRick Saez (01:24): I'm here a lot. Yep. I'm excited to be here today too. I've been following your show for a while, so this is going to be fun. We got to do a reverse. The roles here coming up too. It gets you in the hot seat.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:32): Oh, man. I don't know. That's a lot of pressure. Nah, nah, I feel bad sometimes I feel like I get really used to asking people questions, and then when I get asked questions I'm like, oh, I don't even know how to respond. Not usually the position I'm in.", "token_estimate": 532, "speakers": ["Rick Saez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:02", "end_timestamp": "01:32", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "39b590f2-d4be-4059-ad6b-7c772f3b403d", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Rick Saez (01:47): Right, exactly. Same here. It's been a while since I've been on one, but actually I'm going to be, you're the first of three coming up for me. Guest appearances.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:54): Okay, wonderful. And you've been doing the podcast thing for a while now. You're up to 309 episodes?\n\nRick Saez (02:00): 300. Yeah. I think I recorded 311 last night, but I've only published, we're at 2 98, 300 publishers in a couple of weeks. Yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:10): Wow.\n\nRick Saez (02:10): Yeah, pretty amazing. It's amazing.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:12): That's incredible. I'm sure most folks who are listening right now are familiar with your show. Could you kind of give us a little bit of the background though? How'd you start the podcast?", "token_estimate": 189, "speakers": ["Rick Saez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "01:47", "end_timestamp": "02:12", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "22c3a679-ed19-4737-a562-99025ec43a4f", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Rick Saez (02:22): Yeah, I completely stumbled into it. I was working at LoPro at the time up at Petaluma, living in Alameda. So I had a 45 minute one-way commute and was listening to a lot of podcasts. That's kind of when podcasts were still hitting their stride. And all the major guys we know today, Tim Ferris, pat Flynn, they were all becoming popular. And the one I listened to a lot and still do to this day is the Tim Ferris show. And he was just interviewing the buddies that he runs around with in Silicon Valley and whatever. And one day I'm driving along and I had recently gotten laid off from Low Pro and I thought, what the hell am I going to do now? And then I thought I could do a podcast and just kind of copy what Tim Ferris was doing, just interview all the people I know in the outdoor bits. (03:04): I've been in it for over 20 years and have a lot of great connections and most of the outdoor folks that I've come across to talk and to share their stories. And I think it's a great service to those coming up to hear all the stories of how we got going and how we got started. And I followed the Tim Ferriss model. If you guys are looking to start a podcast, go search Tim Ferriss podcast. And he's got a lot of great feedback on how to get started. Just test drive it. Don't buy a bunch of equipment. I had the equipment from some photography stuff I was doing, tried it out, did a couple episodes and got immediately, got great feedback and I thought, wow, okay, here's the thing. And here we are 300 episodes later, still growing strong\n\nCole Heilborn (03:41): And is every episode, I mean, all the episodes that I've listened to, they follow a very similar format. You chat with folks, you ask 'em how do they get started? What are the things that they've learned? Was it always that way?", "token_estimate": 454, "speakers": ["Rick Saez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "02:22", "end_timestamp": "03:41", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "fcf3fdc2-37c2-478f-8bcf-03594e351b05", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Rick Saez (03:51): Pretty much, yeah. I thought again, on the Tim Ferris side of things, I wanted to share the stories. I think some of the stories and some of the ways that people got into the industry are totally unique. So I think that helps folks coming up. And then just other insights and things that these guys have, because a lot of the folks that I run with, I've been around a long time. I have access to some really great folks that are now in executive roles in the outdoor space. So that's pretty cool. And then also at the end, you got to figure out some ways to monetize. So I like the book question that Tim asks. I'm a big reader and I love finding new books to read. And then you can do an Amazon affiliate link and hopefully make a few bucks that way. And then the gear piece I just came up with, I love to find out all the little gadgets and tools and outdoor things that people use that I may not know about. We don't all know all the cool gear out there. So asking that question I think is just, I've stumbled on some really cool products that way. So yeah, it's pretty fun.\n\nCole Heilborn (04:48): What is not one of your most favorite episodes, but what was one of the most insightful episodes for you that you've done?\n\nRick Saez (04:57): Wow, that's a tough one. There's been so many great ones. Always. It was interesting to talk with. I talked with Tim McGuire for a while. He and I worked together day 16, way back in the day now I think with Eagle Creek still. And just when you talk to someone that you know that close, you learn things you didn't realize. So learning from Tim, a couple things. Same thing with Steve Barker. I worked with Steve Barker for Steve Barker at Eagle Creek for 10 years and learned some things talking to him. And then Doug Robinson and I worked at Mont Bell together, and he was on a satellite phone in Wyoming. We talked for an hour and a half, and it was just a great conversation and just, he brought up stories that I had not known, didn't know how about his family over here in the Eastern share and all that stuff. So he learned something from everybody. But I guess those three stand out.", "token_estimate": 539, "speakers": ["Rick Saez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "03:51", "end_timestamp": "04:57", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "099950b1-6faf-4464-8595-635f4fa4fee9", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Cole Heilborn (05:47): It's funny, I feel like it happens more often than not, where I'll see you publish an episode and that guest either has been on our show or is about to be on our show. I think the most recent one, you put out an episode with Drew Ekins from Hetra. His episode just came in on your show, and he was on our show and he was number 58 for us. So it's funny, I feel like the outdoor industry is big, but it's also small at the same time,\n\nRick Saez (06:16): Especially a podcast. And we were talking about that before we were a couple weeks, or maybe last week when we were setting all this up. There's a remarkably few outdoor podcasts. And a lot of them, I was doing some research, they're all in the hook and bullet world hunt fish stuff. So it's pretty interesting that the outdoor ski adventure space has still got a lot of room to go.\n\nCole Heilborn (06:37): Well, and that kind of gets us into what we're discussing today. No surprise. Rick, you're an expert with podcasts. So we're going to talk about podcasts, this idea of the opportunities out there in the podcast world for brands. A few episodes. We had Emily Holland on the show with the Nature Untold podcast, and she, I think, laid a really great foundation for folks who are trying to get into starting making podcasts. A lot of people like you and I who aren't from a brand or don't have a brand sponsoring us yet or things like that. But what I hope we can kind of dive into today is what does the opportunity look like for brands who are looking at content strategy plans and looking at where do they want to be? Is the podcast world even something that they should be considering? So you alluded to it a little bit, but what is your perspective on the podcast world and brands and how are they interacting with podcasts at this point?", "token_estimate": 456, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Rick Saez"], "start_timestamp": "05:47", "end_timestamp": "06:37", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "063ea331-4724-4a82-b599-b4df944da176", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Rick Saez (07:33): Well, I think first of all, your voice is your most important superpower, I believe, because the human voice is unique in that you can tell inflection, you can tell mood, you can tell all kinds of things just from hearing somebody speak. So I think it's a pretty powerful medium. And the second thing is, as we saw a couple weeks ago when Facebook crashed, your followers can't always read your blog post or watch your video. I mean, if the internet goes down, if they're traveling, if the Facebook goes down, they can't do it. Or the other thing about our, it's unique to the outdoor space, I think because we are so busy, we are so active, we're out fishing, hiking, biking, that we don't want to take the time to watch a blog or a video or whatever, read a blog. But as you're out there on your mountain bike ride, you can stick an earbud in your ear and listen to podcasts all day long that you download the day before. So I think for an active outdoor audience podcast for a fantastic medium, and brands have so many different topics and stories to tell that I think it should be part of everybody's workflow.\n\nCole Heilborn (08:37): I was thinking about what brands are in the podcast world currently, and the one that came to mind was REI. They have their Camp Monsters and their Wildfire podcast. And then I think another one that folks could probably rattle off the tips of their tongue is the Dirt Bag Diaries. They're sponsored by Patagonia and I think Kuat. But how many brands are actually creating their own shows versus sponsoring and partnering with existing shows?", "token_estimate": 400, "speakers": ["Rick Saez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "07:33", "end_timestamp": "08:37", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "ae4df656-19c3-4bc1-8154-cfb61362a82c", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Rick Saez (09:01): That's a good question. I did a search the other day and I looked at, I just did on the Apple Podcast, which is probably not the best place, best app to search on because they sometimes link everything by the terminology. But I just came up with 102 outdoor related podcasts, and I didn't see one other than the Patagonia and outside and REI podcasts that were from a brand or from a retailer. And so I think there's very few, and they're really simple to do. REI also has the wild ideas worth living that Shelby Stinger started, which is I think the first one that they did. I found that early on. But I think it's just, again, it's back to the active lifestyle of your followers and audience can't always, and some of these brands and some of these retailers have great websites and great YouTube content. I mean, it's phenomenal, but they can't always see that. So if you add a podcast and sometimes repurposing a YouTube channel could be your podcast because a lot of that has audio to it. So it's really simple. I think as you've discovered, and I've discovered when we got into this, if we can do it, anybody can do it. And the equipment is, I have an a TR 2100 mic that costs me under a hundred bucks. So you get a mic in Zoom and headsets and away you go. So pretty simple to get started.\n\nCole Heilborn (10:17): Yeah, that's a great point. So if it is so simple though, why are there only a handful of brands that are actually out there creating podcasts?\n\nRick Saez (10:24): Well, I think it's another thing to add to the to-do list, right? I mean, I think it's a combination of, I'm worried that it's going to take up a bunch of time, which it's another thing to do, but as a brand or a retailer, you can hand that off to an employee or an associate and let them run with it, and then maybe it could be a different person every week or a different person every month. And if you're a brand, you get a product manager or someone. This week we're talking about packs, and this week we're talking about footwear and so forth. So there's just a lot of ways to cut that up so that it's not as daunting a secondary task to add your to-do list.", "token_estimate": 542, "speakers": ["Rick Saez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "09:01", "end_timestamp": "10:24", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "2f04ed3c-6a07-4794-aaa4-b2e5aef40226", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Cole Heilborn (10:57): Is that something that you went through when you started your show? Did you imagine it would be this kind of behemoth that you had to tackle, and then as you got into it, did it get easier? Did it get harder?\n\nRick Saez (11:07): I found it pretty simple right out of the gate. I mean, I think maybe I'm kind of a tech wonk, so I was early adopter of Macintosh and all these things, and as a photographer, I've got all kinds of technology to deal with editing and whatnot. So it was relatively simple. The Adobe audition workflow fit right into some of the other Adobe workflows, which was pretty simple to do. And you got to remember the time I was laid off, so I had nothing but time so I could mess around with it and figure it out.\n\nCole Heilborn (11:36): So I think about the concept of storytelling and how important storytelling is for brands in the outdoor space. And you go to a YouTube channel of any brand, and they're trying to pump out, a lot of them are trying to pump out story focused content. And I think for an obvious reason, story is such a great way to connect with people. People in the outdoor industry, we love stories. We love those campfire stories. The over the beer story, rehashing the latest adventure. The podcast seems like such a great format to continuing those stories. And as you said, opens up ways that people can connect with these stories and these brands.", "token_estimate": 355, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Rick Saez"], "start_timestamp": "10:57", "end_timestamp": "11:36", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "98db6091-fd7c-494f-836b-f379d2f9963f", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Rick Saez (12:13): It opens up ways that they connect. And again, it's back to that human voice thing. I mean, I think there's nothing, it's one thing to read a book, to read a blog post, but you don't really get the passion and the excitement or the trepidation that comes with the audio, the audio pieces. Man, you really know that that guy's having fun or you really can tell that, ah, he's a little sketched out here going over that jump or whatever he's doing. And I think that's one piece, because people, we all react to that even as we're sitting in a line talking to one another, you really responding to the voice, not necessarily the eye movement or some of those other things. So the voice is so powerful. It's the first thing a child hears too. The first thing they hear is the mother's voice in their womb. So it's just right out of the gate, we're looking for it and listening for it.\n\nCole Heilborn (13:00): It's interesting that the podcast format works so well because I think about for the most point, it's only audio, right? There's no video, and we're such visual people. I mean, humans are visual learners. That's how we interact with the world. But the podcast works so well. I'm curious why, I think you mentioned something interesting there, this idea that from the beginning of life, all we hear is audio, and then we learn to use vision along the way. Why are podcasts so successful now? Does it have something to do with that? Why has it only just become a thing recently that we're starting to see pop up?", "token_estimate": 384, "speakers": ["Rick Saez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "12:13", "end_timestamp": "13:00", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "931ab6db-e999-44ee-8f6f-e35792584d37", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Rick Saez (13:39): Well, I think it's a combination of they're new. They're still relatively new, even though there's millions of 'em out there. But it's also society has become so busy. We're on the go all the time. And again, that's more important I think, with the outdoor space because not only are we busy on the go with work life and just life, but then the recreational activities we choose take us outside away from the screen. And more and more of us are looking for ways to get away from that screen because screen time has been going up like crazy. And so the podcast is a way to do that. It gets you away from the screen. It allows you to do a couple things at once. And the other thing about a podcast is it's very portable. You can put it on your phone, and we all have our phone in our pocket almost twenty four seven. So as long as you take the time to download or set it up to automatically download, your episodes are right there in your pocket. It's like Steve Jobs said when he introduced the iPod, there's a million songs in your pocket. Well, now there's a million stories in your pocket. So I think that's one of the ways that it's impacting people.\n\nCole Heilborn (14:47): What are some of the less obvious opportunities that you see brands could be pursuing in the podcast space?\n\nRick Saez (14:54): What do you mean by less obvious?\n\nCole Heilborn (14:56): Well, I think, so we talked,\n\nRick Saez (14:57): You mean like types of podcasts\n\nCole Heilborn (14:58): Maybe? Yeah, we talked about storytelling. That seems like a really obvious option, but what are some things that maybe aren't as common that we could start to see?", "token_estimate": 413, "speakers": ["Rick Saez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "13:39", "end_timestamp": "14:58", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "73eb18dd-f34f-4d24-a1e9-916cc48067ef", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Rick Saez (15:08): Well, it's storytelling. It's talking about new products that you've got in the shop or you're going to launch. It's education. A lot of podcasts are educational, so you can learn how to set up your tent, even though it's not visual. You can walk somebody through how to set up their tent or how to pack their backpack or how to, you could guide 'em down a trail. They could be walking down a trail and think, if you're a retailer here in the Eastern, Sierra and Bishop, and there's all this public lands around us with all these trails, and those of us that have been here for a while have been on some of these trails. What we could do in our sleep, blindfold me, spin me around, and I can probably find my way up to Southlake or to Northlake or to one of the back Country Lakes, Humphreys Lakes basin. (15:50): I could probably do that blindfolded, and you could do a podcast about that trail, hiking up that trail. I could start at the trail head, here's where you are. Here's what you need to plan for. Don't go hard right out of the gate because it's a little swampy, whatever it might be. And they could have that in their ear, and then you could be talking about features and things they're seeing as they're walking along the way. So I think it's, again, that story, that voice and that audio piece of it just makes it unlimited. Same with photography. You could go through photography and some guy's going to go up and shoot the beautiful fall color at North Lake, and you could guide him up the road. Here's where you drive, here's where you park. There's three options to go, or there's unlimited options to go take the photo. But if you want to get this, look, you can go down there at this time of day with this light. Here's your F-stop and here's your shutter speed. Just point and shoot. It could be that simple or it could get more complex.", "token_estimate": 470, "speakers": ["Rick Saez"], "start_timestamp": "15:08", "end_timestamp": "15:08", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "9e1913bb-d6a9-4755-b3b8-ba07f55d3f9d", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Cole Heilborn (16:41): Oh, that's interesting. So I feel like if you're telling a story on a podcast, the guest is listening in, they're sitting in the audience listening to the story. What you're describing is almost more of the podcast is accompanying this person on the adventure. They're out there with them together. I've never considered the podcast format to take that shape before.\n\nRick Saez (17:04): I don't know that it has. I mean, haven't heard of any. I think some of the cooking podcasts might do that. I don't know. I haven't listened to many of those, but I can't believe it's not done. It's just another one of those things that I think you can do, because again, you can talk. It's just talk anybody through anything, whether you're teaching them something, whether you're just sharing a story, whatever it is. So that's what the podcast can do. You can really be in somebody's ear talking to them about anything.\n\nCole Heilborn (17:29): That's interesting. As you're talking, I'm imagining what is a content series that would be really valuable and informative for people as they're outside? What if someone made a video about, or not a video, excuse me. What if someone made a podcast that went through all the 10 essentials and how do you use them while you're out in the field? And people could pre-download them, and let's say they're out there and they need a refresher on orientation and their compass. You could pop that in. That would be cool.", "token_estimate": 368, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Rick Saez"], "start_timestamp": "16:41", "end_timestamp": "17:29", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "9ff29bc5-a149-488b-bd56-d3b807e1f813", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Rick Saez (18:00): Nailed it. Exactly. Yeah. And it could go so far as we're going to teach you how to hike the hike, the Humphreys Basin in your backpack down free pacing for two days, so it could walk 'em up that trail as you come out to the top, if you look over the scenery, you're going to walk to the right, you're going to see this flat spot, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah. You get 'em there. Now we're going to set up our tent and you can walk 'em through. You lay out your tent, you set out your stakes. You just walk 'em through that whole thing. And then, okay, it tenses up. Now it's about time to cook dinner. So let's light the stove. I don't know, you might have to have some audio or some visuals with that a little bit on some of that instructional stuff. Depends on how intricate it gets, but it really could be that simple. Just tell 'em how to do it.\n\nCole Heilborn (18:44): I'm almost imagining when you go to a museum and you pick up that headset when you walk in the door, and then you kind of go around and press, I want to listen to number 7 37 or whatever. And then it kind of gives you a history, and the voice walks you through the history of what the display you're looking at. What if you could choose top 10 hikes? And then someone, a brand, let's say, could create five episodes that explain the topography or how this landscape was formed. And so you reach the bluegrass lookout and you queue up that episode, and then for 10 minutes you listen to someone talk to you about how was this valley formed?\n\nRick Saez (19:25): Yeah, it could be all the geology and all the geography. It doesn't have to be that minute in details of the educational piece. Could be, you could be sitting around your camp at night and Hey, I'm looking at these stars. What am I seeing?\n\nCole Heilborn (19:36): Or what, what's the floor and fauna around me? What is this tree?\n\nRick Saez (19:41): Exactly. Yeah, it's really unlimited. I think, again, back to that story piece, we tell stories. I don't even think we realize sometimes the stories that we're telling that could be turned into a podcast. Yeah. Yeah.", "token_estimate": 530, "speakers": ["Rick Saez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "18:00", "end_timestamp": "19:41", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "f92b5b79-572c-425d-a177-852ab0f7713c", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Cole Heilborn (19:54): I mean, in the last three minutes, I feel like we've come up with some pretty good ideas. Why don't you start working on these?\n\nRick Saez (20:01): Well, I got plenty of things going on. I got my show going on. I actually do have a couple of ideas for a couple of other shows. One I've been toying around with this called the 3 95 Chronicles, and I've got a graphic and I've got, I think one episode in the Can already, but over here in Bishop Highway 3 95 runs up and down the Owens Valley and comes from way South, runs up and down California, really. But the outdoor piece of it starts in the southern end of this valley and goes all the way up through Reno. And as you drive that road, there's not only beautiful scenery, but there's all kinds of cool little towns that you drive through. And my thought is to just go into all these little towns and interview the outdoor shop owner, the fly fishing shop owner, the guy who's got the best burritos in town. (20:47): You want to stop at this coffee shop because blah, blah, blah, blah, blah. And a lot of them have been there for a while. Some of 'em are very, very old since this road was paved maybe. And so there's some history there that can go along with that. And I think that would be just a fun, not only for someone coming up here to do an outdoor thing, but if you're just traveling along the highway, because I remember as a kid, there used to be this booklet that you could get, and as you're driving north, you looked on the left hand side of the pages and it had all the outlines of the skyline and what peaks you were seeing and whatnot. And then as you're driving south, you looked on the other side and it showed you all that. So that's where the idea came from. But again, the audio piece to share all these guys' stories, I think it'd be fun.\n\nCole Heilborn (21:27): Oh my goodness. Yeah. What if there was an RV company out there and they made a route guide for all of the major highways, like Route 66? You could have an entire journey down that highway.", "token_estimate": 513, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Rick Saez"], "start_timestamp": "19:54", "end_timestamp": "21:27", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "073b5a6b-c46b-409a-973b-266623422ff0", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Rick Saez (21:41): There is a guy that has a Route 66 podcast. He was on my show a while back. Yeah, I forget the name of it. I should have looked that up. But\n\nCole Heilborn (21:48): Was it this idea?\n\nRick Saez (21:50): No, no, no. He just goes on these adventures. He does adventure travel trips on Route 66, so he'll get a bunch of people together and guide him down Route 66, kind of this historical thing. Pretty cool. Pretty fun.\n\nCole Heilborn (22:01): Wow. Oh, man.\n\nRick Saez (22:03): And it's unlimited. I mean, that's the thing. It's just like anything that you can come up with. It could be a foodie thing along Highway 3 95 or the Barbecue Highway down in, go down to Texas somewhere, and it's just all the barbecue places. I mean, it could be, it's really unlimited.\n\nCole Heilborn (22:18): And what I like about these ideas is the audience is extremely focused. So you're providing, ideally, a bunch of value for very specific targeted audience. And it's less of this spray and pray idea that I think we see happening a lot.", "token_estimate": 260, "speakers": ["Rick Saez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "21:41", "end_timestamp": "22:18", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "21212f3b-61ed-474e-b2f8-1400df00ecdf", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Rick Saez (22:36): Well, and that kind of goes back to one of the things that I teach in my course is you've really got to hone in on your ideal listener, because I dunno if a lot of people can look up a thousand True Fans by Kevin Kelly. I dunno if you've read that, but he wrote this piece. Kevin Kelly is one of the founders of Wired Magazine, and he wrote this piece on a thousand True Fans. And how he says that if you have a thousand people that consume everything you make, you can make a pretty damn good living. Because if you sell something or advertise something and those thousand people spend 50 bucks with you a month or whatever, there's some pretty good money. And so if you really hone in on that ideal listener, A, it helps you figure out and focus on the content. And B, it guarantees that you're going to have a good qualified audience to follow. And an audience is different than a follower. A follower will follow you and maybe not always tap in, but an audience will come back for more. They know what you're offering, they know what you're delivering. My audience comes back every Tuesday because they've heard 300 episodes and they know it's going to be another one with some outdoor person. They like to hear that. So audience is, I think, more valuable than followers.\n\nCole Heilborn (23:46): I guess there is an important distinction between those two.\n\nRick Saez (23:50): You want the guys that are going to pay for the ticket to come in the concert, not the groupie that's going to show up and buy the t-shirt and not come in, wait for the CD to come out.\n\nCole Heilborn (23:57): Sure. And honestly, I think that's some encouragement for people who have started podcasts and they're trying to figure out, how am I doing download wise? You look at your downloads and you're like, oh man, I feel like I should be doing way better. But how many of those people are the right audience, are the right people you want to be talking to? And it sounds like that's something you practice or you preach in your COING programs.", "token_estimate": 512, "speakers": ["Rick Saez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "22:36", "end_timestamp": "23:57", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "9c9e9698-c827-4cd1-9e1f-ccb861210b84", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Rick Saez (24:22): Yeah, I preach that in my educational stuff and I practice it too. I can't tell you the times that I've been talking to somebody like we're talking today and that we'll be wandering off topic a little bit and I want to get back on track. And for whatever reason, my ideal listener pops into my head. And I mean, when you create your ideal listener, you want to know what color shoes he wears, I mean, right down to really get into the details. And it helps a lot. And I think the other thing about audiences, I think sponsors will find an audience more valuable than downloads if you're only talking to 500 people a month. I think that's the magic number. They want 500 downloads a month, and that's a good number. But some people don't always get that. And I think that's within the first, these metrics have changed a bit, but I think it's 500 downloads in the first 30 days is kind of the magic number. (25:15): I'll have to look that up and get back to you to confirm that. But as a sponsor, that could be 500 people, but maybe only a hundred people are going to be interested in my product. Whereas if you have a hundred people that are all interested in my product, that's more valuable to me as a sponsor than 500 people that might not be. And so that's one of the things that I'm trying to confirm with everybody too, is look, my audience is X, Y, z. I did a survey a few months ago on just trying to find out who it was. It just verified that it is the outdoor industry. It's all the folks that we see at the Outdoor Retailer Show. But now I can go to people like some of these tourism folks around the tourism boards. They're interested in talking to me because the one guy I was talking to in New Mexico, he wants to get some of these outdoor brands to go down there and do some of their photo shoots. They got beautiful country, beautiful outdoor activities, come down here and do a photo shoot. So there's a lot of different ways to cut that. But yeah,", "token_estimate": 496, "speakers": ["Rick Saez"], "start_timestamp": "24:22", "end_timestamp": "24:22", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "70a24f90-e289-4612-86dc-feb477498e6a", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Cole Heilborn (26:11): So for marketers that are listening and they're trying to figure out, how do I start? Maybe they just took one of our ideas and they're going to pay us a nice commission on one of our ideas we came up with, and they're going to make it a reality. If they came to you, how would you walk 'em through? How do you get started?\n\nRick Saez (26:28): Well, the first thing is the ideal listener. That's the first piece of homework I make 'em do. Then once you figure out what you're going to talk to them about and who it is you're talking to, then we can go into the formal creation of a podcast. All the, you need an intro, you need an outro, you need this, you need that. Talk about the equipment, all those kinds of things. But I really want you to know who it is you're talking to and why you're talking to before we do anything. Because if you don't have that, you can easily get off track two episodes in, and then you're one of the pod theater guys that did two episodes and you're gone. And I think it's so valuable. I hate to see people lose that\n\nCole Heilborn (27:07): If they have no idea how to quantify success, and you are trying to help them understand what would success look like for this new podcast? How do you think about that? What does success look like for you in your own show?", "token_estimate": 330, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Rick Saez"], "start_timestamp": "26:11", "end_timestamp": "27:07", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "28a4b0ee-efe8-4b41-a6cc-5710a4c45312", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Rick Saez (27:21): That's a tricky one. I think for me, a lot of the success is based on, I mean, I would probably do it for free. I didn't anticipate having so much fun doing it. And it just, because I get to talk to all these great people, I'm like, Charles Caral or something. I mean, it's so much fun to talk to these people and hear the stories that I didn't know about. So I'd probably do it for free. I'd like to monetize it, but I'd probably do it for free. But I think success, it depends on what you're doing it for. I'm doing it to share all these stories. So that's the success piece for me. If it does turn into a monetization opportunity and I can quit a day job and just do this all the better. But I think I would still share these stories because there's so many great stories of, and I think you learn from those that came before you, whatever it is you're doing. (28:09): So I think it's that valuable. But everybody's got their own thing. So I would ask them first, you got to do something that you're going to like and enjoy, so you stick with it. So whether it's hobby based or something you're having expertise at, and as long as you're still liking it and enjoying it, keep doing it. It takes a while. You can't just throw it up there and start talking about it and five episodes in, okay, I want that. Where's that money? That's not how it works. People have to get you, find you and you have to market it and get it out to all the places where your people are. So that's the advice I give people.\n\nCole Heilborn (28:40): YouTube in the last 10 years has become a really popular and some might say necessary channel to be on. Do you feel like the podcast format, apple Podcast, Spotify, will eventually reach YouTube status as it is today?", "token_estimate": 441, "speakers": ["Rick Saez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "27:21", "end_timestamp": "28:40", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "1bf5c7bc-7c3e-4579-932b-31e5193720c2", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Rick Saez (28:54): Oh, yeah. Yep. I think so. I think it's already happened. I mean, I think it's not as big as YouTube yet, but I think it's getting there because you look at all the new apps. I mean, when I started this, there weren't half the apps or half the destinations to push the show out to. I host on Lipson, and Lipson just had a new destination called Ghana, which is in India. So you can push your show to India, and I get a handful of downloads every week from India, which is like, that's very cool. So I think it's becoming pretty global. I think it is global, international. It'll get there. And my show goes to YouTube. I don't do the video piece of it, but there's just a graphic up there. And for those of you that get all your content on YouTube, a lot of people do these days. You can go listen to podcasts on YouTube. A lot of 'em are there.\n\nCole Heilborn (29:42): Yeah. How important is that when considering your content ecosystem strategy?\n\nRick Saez (29:49): Well, yeah, the YouTube component itself is not that important. But the importance is, I tell everybody you want to and need to be everywhere. Because the thing about podcasts is it's not like YouTube. There are multiple destinations. The reason Apple's so big is because the Apple Podcast app is native on the iPhone. That's why Apple's and Mac have kind of dominated podcasts, the Android phones and all that stuff. You have to go download Spotify or Stitcher or Pandora or wherever it is you want to listen. Apple had a built in and they created podcasts way back in the day. So that helps. But I think you just have to be everywhere because of that. You never know what people are going to be listening to, how they're going to be listening. So you want to be everywhere, and Lipson makes that easy.\n\nCole Heilborn (30:34): And Lipson is just, I mean, that's the podcast hosting site that we use. There's a few others out there I can think of. Anchor is Sprout Bean, is that one", "token_estimate": 493, "speakers": ["Rick Saez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "28:54", "end_timestamp": "30:34", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "8650c07c-c9f5-41ec-89e7-11e091813358", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Rick Saez (30:43): Pod Bean? I think it is. Yeah. There's a handful. Yeah, I just signed on with a new production outfit. They host stuff too, but I'm not sure how popular that is. Lipson is, again, to me, I always want to be with the best. And the guys Lipson is great too, because they have a great podcast themselves called The Feed, and I learned so much from those guys because they publish all the shows, host all the shows, they're on the front lines. Rob Walsh, the VP of podcasting relationships or something, I forget, sorry Rob, I forget your title, but he's always talking to Apple and Google and all these folks. He's on the inside track. He knows all these things ahead of time. He can tell you, Hey, this is what's coming up. Heads up. Yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (31:27): Well, if Lipson's listening, hopefully you guys could update your website and make it look a little more modern.\n\nRick Saez (31:33): Oh, they're doing it. They're doing it Lips in Five.\n\nCole Heilborn (31:35): Okay. That's right. It's in beta, right?\n\nRick Saez (31:38): It might still be in beta. It might be launched, I'm not sure, but yeah, yeah, yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (31:42): That's my only beef.\n\nRick Saez (31:44): It's significantly updated. Yeah, they're doing a good job of that. And I don't use their web, I don't use, they have a place where you can use their hosting to host a website for you. I don't do that. I have my own website. Is that what you're talking about? Yeah, that is pretty outdated.\n\nCole Heilborn (31:58): And then just their interface, I feel like in the actual Lipson app. You mentioned that you started working with a production company. Are they helping you produce or manage the podcast? What does that look like?", "token_estimate": 433, "speakers": ["Rick Saez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "30:43", "end_timestamp": "31:58", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "af6fb25b-0d4e-415d-82b7-b92375b5e7aa", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 21, "content": "Rick Saez (32:11): Yeah, they're taking some of the work off of me to get the production side of it done, which that's not why I hired them because the production, I have my workflow pretty tight. But what they also do is they turn the show notes and they create a blog post out of the show notes, and they optimize it for SEO purposes, so it's more likely to get found by Google and all that stuff. So it's a pretty detailed show notes page with all kinds of links, and they research the terminology. I might say, Hey, we're talking to Cole about podcasting today, and that'll be the title. And they'll go, no, no, no, that's not a good title. They'll tweak the title and make it all SEO appropriate. So they do all that stuff. Yeah, that's what's really been valuable to me.\n\nCole Heilborn (32:53): Oh, interesting. So are they creating transcripts of every episode and then optimizing those or is it like a synopsis?\n\nRick Saez (33:03): I dunno how they do it like a blog post. It's like a true article well written. It's well optimized. There's links to everything. Yeah, I'm assuming they transcribe it and then they rewrite it, and I'm sure it's all done in Asia. I don't know. But yeah, just stumbled on 'em. I signed on initially with 'em to go back and republish, I think it's 15 episodes, show notes. So they created blog posts at all that, and then I just produced five episodes with them, or I think it was five episodes. I'm going to produce five more with them just to have them do all the audio and all that stuff. It does take a lot of work, and it frees me up to do other things like workshops and that kind of stuff.\n\nCole Heilborn (33:46): Sure. So they're editing episodes for you and Okay, awesome. That's great.\n\nRick Saez (33:52): And they have all kinds of cool, they have time to do all kinds of cool social graphics. My social graphics, were getting a little tired, so they come up with some cool things and clickbait terminology so that someone wants to say, Hey, here, what Drew and said this week about Seth Godin. There's little cliquey things like that, which I don't even know how to do.", "token_estimate": 530, "speakers": ["Rick Saez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "32:11", "end_timestamp": "33:52", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "532b4afb-e977-4454-b729-7fce08d9e368", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 22, "content": "Cole Heilborn (34:12): Well, that's a good point. If you're going to start a podcast, you can do it by yourself, but you don't have to. There's plenty of resources out there. And maybe if you don't have in-house production capabilities, there's some places out there that you can find resources. What's the place you work with and are there some others out there that you'd recommend?\n\nRick Saez (34:31): Yeah, the one I'm working with now is called Po Ize, P-O-D-E-T-I-Z-E. I was using Resonate for a while, resonate just for producing episodes when I kind of got in a jam, but there's a million of 'em Resonate PO ize. I mean, just look up podcast production and you'll find all kinds of places. But I also strongly recommend everybody to do your first 10 episodes yourself, because you have to know it's better if you know how the soup is cooked so that when something goes wrong, you can fix it because something does go wrong. It's just the nature of the beast. Somebody either their side crashed or the guy was sick that day, or they flubbed, they missed this. I got to go in and edit this out because they missed it. Whatever it is, you got to know how to go in there and do that. So I always tell people, you should produce your first 10, five to 10 that way. What's going on?\n\nCole Heilborn (35:23): That's a great idea. As a host, do you listen to your episodes after they're recorded to see if you can improve?\n\nRick Saez (35:31): Not always. I try to. I use Script and I read them a lot to you. Descrip is a transcription. It transcribes the audio, and I just read, I can find faster, and I take pretty detailed notes as I'm interviewing. So I write down the timestamp. Oh, I just flubbed that up here so I can go back and look at, I don't have to listen to the whole thing. I know we screwed up at 1922 and 42, 48. I can go back and clean that up.\n\nCole Heilborn (35:58): If you had to make a prediction about how the podcast world is going to change or what sort of opportunities it's going to open up that people might not be expecting or even aware of. Do you have any predictions? They could be wild predictions. It doesn't have to be factual.", "token_estimate": 540, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Rick Saez"], "start_timestamp": "34:12", "end_timestamp": "35:58", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "5430b70d-139c-4db7-9252-9a09ee9649c7", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 23, "content": "Rick Saez (36:17): Wild predictions. Wow, that's a good one. I think it's really not a prediction. It's more of a, it opens opportunities that none of us see coming, whether it's an opportunity to work with another company on some other project that they're doing, whether somebody wants you to write a book, somebody like what you said about this, they want to dig into your thoughts about that more deeply. I mean, I just think it opens up so many doors that I think that's going to continue, and I think it's just going to continue to grow. I think it's going to be, I mean, it might be replace radio is what I'm thinking. Radio, I don't even listen to radio anymore. I have XM radio, so I just listen to music. So I think this could be podcasts, could be a channel on XM radio, for example, you could go to XM channel 1 97, and it's the outdoor podcast channel, and it's the tennis podcast channel, whatever it might be. That'd be kind of cool, actually.\n\nCole Heilborn (37:11): That would be,\n\nRick Saez (37:12): You hear that XM give me a call.\n\nCole Heilborn (37:16): Yeah, you could have a whole bunch of scheduled episodes just playing throughout the day, and you just tune into the channel and away you go.\n\nRick Saez (37:25): Yeah, and I think that's sort of already exists. I think that's a lot of the way people find shows when you go into my show on Apple. You can just scroll through and listen to what you want to listen to or put it on play 'em all.\n\nCole Heilborn (37:36): You mentioned that there's a lot of hook and bullet podcasts out there, and of the a hundred some that you found in the outdoors, a lot of 'em were that nature. Why is that? Any ideas?\n\nRick Saez (37:48): I don't know. That's a good question. You'd think that there would be as many other mountain biking or backpacking. I don't know. That's a good question.\n\nCole Heilborn (37:59): I wonder if it's because that demographic really embraces the storytelling. The fish was this big, and then the next day it was a little bigger type of story. So maybe", "token_estimate": 511, "speakers": ["Rick Saez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "36:17", "end_timestamp": "37:59", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "c772a52c-cf1c-4aca-94a0-be1488300917", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 24, "content": "Rick Saez (38:13): It could be that, and it could be too. I think fishermen and hunters, they always like to know where to go. So I want to go, we're the big fish. So they talk about, I think, I haven't listened to very many of these, but they do describe the hunt or the fish. And we got on this boat down in Baja at Loretto, and we went to, here's the settings on our compass, and we fished and caught a ton of Dorado, or I was hiking in the Wind River ranges chasing a moose, and we found them at this spot, and this is how we snuck up. All that kind of things. There's a lot of more how to, I think. But the same could be said for hikers and skiers and mountain bikers and all that. The PCT could be, imagine the whole PCT. Oh man, that'd be a great podcast actually.\n\nCole Heilborn (38:58): I mean, if there's anyone doing the PCT right now, maybe you just grab a mic and start interviewing people you pass along the way.\n\nRick Saez (39:05): Wouldn't that be fun?\n\nCole Heilborn (39:05): That would be incredible\n\nRick Saez (39:07): Stories of the PCT. I know a guy that wrote a book. He was on my show recently too. But yeah, that'd be a cool podcast.\n\nCole Heilborn (39:13): My wife is going to be really frustrated with me. She did the Washington section of the PCT and Are they Trail Angels? Is that what they're called? Sounds like. I don't know a whole lot about that culture, but it sounds like that's a really, that could be a really interesting series. Like doing episodes on these kind of renowned trail angels.\n\nRick Saez (39:37): Yep. Or the Trail Angel could do episodes on the through hikers that come through. Right. Both of those would be good shows. Yeah, and the PCT association could be the sponsor. There you go.\n\nCole Heilborn (39:50): And I think when you consider the amount of production that goes into a podcast, comparing this to a film or a video series, that's what we do. We create videos and it's so much less work, and you still achieve incredible quality. So as you're considering, as we're talking about this, I'm like, huh, what would it take to create a Trail Angel video series versus a Trail Angel podcast series? And the lift is so much smaller.", "token_estimate": 547, "speakers": ["Rick Saez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "38:13", "end_timestamp": "39:50", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "eb77e428-643b-4ea8-a61a-d3be7508cea5", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 25, "content": "Rick Saez (40:18): Oh, you need your earbuds and your phone and you're done. You can record it on your phone, talk it on your phone, do a recording, have your budds, then you download that, edit it up and audition or something. Or just publish it raw. Maybe you don't need to clean it up, just publish it and have a host. You can find, I mean, Lipson, I have one of their, I don't have their basic package. It's like 20 bucks a month, so that's nothing to host. So yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (40:43): How important is the host, if it's a great story in your role, how do you see your role in your podcast?\n\nRick Saez (40:53): I think that your host is pretty important because that's where it all starts. That's where your show lives. So you want to make sure that they don't have continued technical problems where all of a sudden they didn't publish or they were down for a week or whatever. I mean, that's what I love about Lipson is those guys are all in, they are connected to Apple and Google and all the folks, and they talk with them frequently and they know what's coming down or when something's going to be updated. I mean, when Apple had that big update recently and kind of crashed a bunch of shows and muck things up for a few weeks there, Lipson was all over it. They were on it. They were working with Apple to figure it out and fix it. And so yeah, I think it's really critical to have a good host. (41:33): And at 20 bucks a month, I dunno, again, I'm sorry about all the other hosts out there that I'm not familiar with. I should do a little more research for some of my training, but there's a lot of host places to host. You can self-host I think as well. And there's also ways to, I was interviewed, I was at the Outdoor Writers Association of America Conference last or two weeks ago in Vermont, and Mandela has a podcast. I met Mandela at that show, and she also repurposes her podcast on our radio station in Missoula. So that's another opportunity to spread the word.\n\nCole Heilborn (42:07): So as again, marketers are trying to figure out how do we start a podcast? How important is the anchor or the\n\nRick Saez (42:17): Host,", "token_estimate": 537, "speakers": ["Rick Saez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "40:18", "end_timestamp": "42:17", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "9a487702-838e-43c7-8fc8-bf6b99b09f65", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 26, "content": "Cole Heilborn (42:18): The host, the person, the voice, who's kind of guiding the show? How important is that component?\n\nRick Saez (42:25): Well, I think it depends on what kind of thing you want to do. So if I was a retailer, for example, I would start a podcast and I, I might have the store owner as the host, but only once in a while. So that all that pressure's not on that person to host every episode because you can have, in your store, you have the guy who's the stove expert, so you're going to have him do a month long series once a week on backpacking stoves, and the host, the owner can tap in every once in a while and make some comments. But then Jim or Bill, or whoever it is, the stove expert, can be the guy who's doing every episode about the stoves. He knows all the intricate how to fix 'em, how to do all these things. So I think it's important, but it's not as important as people think. People might think that, yeah, I got to have a good voice and blah, blah, blah, blah, blah. And I don't know. We all have our voice. That's the beauty of podcasting too. It's so available to everybody. It really doesn't matter. I have a face for radio anyway.\n\nCole Heilborn (43:28): That's the beauty right now. No one can see our faces. That's right. When you were getting started, how did you practice? How did you get warmed up to host a podcast? Did you do any training? Did you watch YouTube videos? Did you just trial and error it?", "token_estimate": 363, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Rick Saez"], "start_timestamp": "42:18", "end_timestamp": "43:28", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "9754dda0-b497-49a5-98f8-62e491d3576a", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 27, "content": "Rick Saez (43:44): I did it. I just jumped in and I think my background as a sales guy helped me a lot because as a sales guy, I was used to traveling around the country walking into these shops and asking a bunch of questions. So yeah, it was pretty simple. And again, I had so many friends that I could talk to, so I just talked to a couple buddies, said, Hey, I'm going to call you on the phone. I got all this set up. So I called and we talked and away we went. Yeah, I jumped in with both feet. And again, I followed the Tim Ferriss model too, where I didn't invest in a bunch of equipment. I used equipment I already had. All I did was buy a get a Lipson account. So I had somewhere to put the podcast when I was done. That was 20 bucks, I think. Then they have a free one, I think maybe. Anyway, it didn't cost me any money at all. Like I said, I had of the equipment just did it and away we went. And I was just kind of shocked too, because the first handful I pushed out, a couple people called or emailed, said, dude, you're great at this. How'd you figure this out? I'm like, I stumbled into it. I have no idea. Literally started doing it, and here we are. So anybody can do it. Yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (44:54): Cool. Did you by chance, listen to Tim Ferriss's recent two episodes ago, the podcast you put out about how he started his show?", "token_estimate": 337, "speakers": ["Rick Saez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "43:44", "end_timestamp": "44:54", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "040e9d86-a099-402f-86ee-9abef192f2e3", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 28, "content": "Rick Saez (45:03): Yeah, I was going to mention that he has a new one out now. It's about three hours long. It's really long, but his earlier one is good as well. But I think it's with Nick Hutchins, and I'm only maybe an hour and a half into it, but it's just again, more Tim Ferriss. I mean, he jumps in with both feet and studies everything and gets all into the weeds so he can hack it is why he does that. He's a great hacker. He kind of finds shortcuts and stuff, but it's just an update of all the things that he did way back when, and he said, the first one I listened to, it's probably still available on his blogger, on his podcast somewhere. Don't spend a bunch of money. Don't buy a bunch of equipment. Use what you have practice do. Just get in and talk into the mic, and you're going to hate your own voice. Everybody hates their own voice. It is what it is. But interview, tell stories. Do whatever you want to do and see if you'll like it. Because if you don't like it, don't go whole hog. If you've already spent all this equipment and realize, oh, I hate this, that's no good, and we all have you. If you have an iPhone in your pocket instead of earbuds, you're good to go.\n\nCole Heilborn (46:07): Yeah, well said. We're kind of coming up on the end of our time for today, I guess. I know.\n\nRick Saez (46:13): Flew by, didn't it?\n\nCole Heilborn (46:14): It did. It really did. I guess you have any other thoughts, any other lasting notes you want to pass along as we've been kind of dancing around a few topics, but", "token_estimate": 384, "speakers": ["Rick Saez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "45:03", "end_timestamp": "46:14", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "8c867721-ae49-41d3-987c-363c84aab50b", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 29, "content": "Rick Saez (46:25): Yeah, a couple things. One is back to what we talked about earlier about podcasts in the outdoor space. Podcasts appeal to a very active audience, and I think it lines right up to the outdoor space and podcasters online, typically listeners, they're more likely to subscribe to Netflix or to Amazon Prime or some of those things. So they're not going to be exposed to radio and television advertising, so they'll be more apt to hear some of, and be open to some of the sponsorship things and reads that you put on your thing. So you got to keep that in mind. And then 45% of monthly podcast listeners have a household income of over $75,000. So it's a pretty affluent group of folks. I dunno if the $75,000 is still affluent, but I think it is for young folks just getting started. So they're more likely to have money to spend doing things, and they're also more likely to follow brands and things that are mentioned on a podcast on social media because they're all over social media. So they have some different habits and demographics that we might not think of as we're starting our podcast. And that's just all from some of the research from Edison Infantile. If you go look up Edison, you can find out all those details.\n\nCole Heilborn (47:36): So you just opened up a whole nother can of worms that we're going to give ourselves another five minutes to keep chatting if that's cool.\n\nRick Saez (47:44): Sorry. Yeah, fine.\n\nCole Heilborn (47:46): No, let's do it. You mentioned sponsorships. How do you begin to think about sponsorships as a podcaster? I'm assuming it's different if you are someone like yourself versus a brand that's creating their own branded podcast. But how do you begin to think about that?", "token_estimate": 435, "speakers": ["Rick Saez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "46:25", "end_timestamp": "47:46", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "80d6c135-5ffd-4dcb-856f-5d26d406ec43", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 30, "content": "Rick Saez (48:01): Well, I think you have to think of sponsorships completely different than you think of advertising my sponsorships. I'm trying to figure out how we can work together to create something that's mutually beneficial, not just something that you're spending money to have your logo on my show because that's boring, that doesn't work for me, that doesn't work for you. What kind of initiative? Are you trying to get more subscribers? Are you trying to sell something? Do you want to get more people to your website? What it is you are trying to accomplish? And then let's come up with something fun. Let's come up with a cool giveaway or cool. Next month actually, when this drops, it'll have already happened, but in the month of November in 2021, I'm working with four wheel campers, and it's a camper that fits in the back, typical camper, but the roof pops up. (48:51): It's a popup camper, but it's like a traditional big camper shell. And he's sponsoring the entire month, and I'm interviewing all of his ambassadors. I shouldn't say all four of his ambassadors. And we're just talking about them. And one guy's a climber, one couple of guys started a clothing company. One guy travels around and does events. He just publishes, produces all these events like the Susan Coleman cancer events and triathlons and all this stuff. And he travels around pop-up camper. So that's a different unique way to have him spend money on content that's going to get probably more valuable than just slapping a logo on my sponsors page. Those are the kinds of things I'm trying to do with sponsorship.\n\nCole Heilborn (49:36): You're to clarify your hosting, these ambassadors telling their story, but then it's all kind of under this umbrella of the company.\n\nRick Saez (49:46): The commonality is they all travel around in a four wheel camp.\n\nCole Heilborn (49:48): Gotcha. And for something like that, I mean, I guess folks could just listen to the episode, but how are you structuring that? Is it following the same pattern as your show?", "token_estimate": 512, "speakers": ["Rick Saez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "48:01", "end_timestamp": "49:48", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "7226d262-5fe0-4d01-b3e7-130e8e8a0c51", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 31, "content": "Rick Saez (49:59): Yep, same pattern. Same. I asked most of the same questions that I typically ask, but then we also talk about their four wheel camper rig. What kind of rig do you have? How'd you get involved with them? Why did you choose them? What are your favorite features? Like one of the guys climbs, he's been a climber for a long time, and he traveled around in his Subaru and he says, I just got tired of living outside. I love climbing, I love being outside, but sometimes at the end of the day, you want to just come home and get in the truck or the shell and make a cup of coffee or a can of soup. You don't have to stand out in the cold and the wind and the rain and get your stove to light and make soup. He says, that's the reason I switched. (50:40): I just want to still be outside. I just don't want to live outside. So those kinds of insights come up. And we didn't really do a lot of prep with his ambassadors because I kind of wanted to feel it out and see how that went. And it turned out great, I think. But those kind of things are just all you can get. Your ambassadors, you can get. I mean, imagine if you had a brand sponsor an episode or four episodes. I try to do four episode minimum. You get the whole month and we talk about your, say it's a multi-product category brand, and you have different product categories. So we get your apparel designer on one episode, we get your PAC designer on another episode, we get your footwear designer on another episode, and you really can dive in to the details of how the ethos of your design and your products and why you do what you do. And that's going to be very valuable as content for consumers that love your brand as opposed to just slapping up a logo on the page and say, this podcast is sponsored, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah. So there's a lot of ways to cut that. So many things you can do. So much fun you can have with it that I think it's pretty cool.\n\nCole Heilborn (51:46): Do you ever imagine the podcast format starting to incorporate a live stream component?", "token_estimate": 515, "speakers": ["Rick Saez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "49:59", "end_timestamp": "51:46", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "4dbc2890-00bf-45fb-93bf-3a687d8a2030", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 32, "content": "Rick Saez (51:54): I think that's already happened. I think some people are doing that. We could be doing this on Facebook live right now recording this podcast episode. And even though the content and everything will be out now, not everybody will see it. So then we could publish it up to Lipson and then it could be a livestream podcast.\n\nCole Heilborn (52:10): Okay. But what about Apple? Could Apple ever incorporate a livestream function so I could open up the podcast live stream it through Apple? Could you see that being a thing?\n\nRick Saez (52:22): I'm sure they could. Yeah, I'm sure they could. Yeah, that could be a thing. Yeah, it'd be another thing for Apple to do. I'm not sure I want Apple to do another thing, but just do what you're doing really well. I keep that up. But yeah, that could totally be a thing.\n\nCole Heilborn (52:37): I feel like one of the beauties of the podcast is that it's such a great intimate way to connect with people. And I'm imagining a lot of the influencers out on YouTube. People love their vlogs because you're kind of tagging along with them. And the YouTube live thing is such a popular experience because you can be there in person. But what if you could do something similar with a podcast where let's say I'm adventuring and I'm driving around in my kitted out van and people are tagging along with me while I'm driving down, let's say Route 66. And it's a live interaction. It's a live engagement q and a thing. I feel like that could be a really cool component as well.\n\nRick Saez (53:28): Yeah, I think you could do that now. I think that's basically just creating your Facebook Live or YouTube video while you're doing it. And then it'd be the reverse of repurposing your podcast, a YouTube, you'd be repurposing your YouTube video to a podcast. It'd be live on YouTube. You could see it, but then again, if somebody wasn't able to see it, you could go watch it later. Yeah. Yeah. That's pretty cool.\n\nCole Heilborn (53:51): So back to the sponsorship thing. How do you feel like audiences respond to kind of the classic bumper mid-roll or end advertisement placement?", "token_estimate": 532, "speakers": ["Rick Saez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "51:54", "end_timestamp": "53:51", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "1c5c91e5-5634-4ef4-a356-3e54a998c263", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 33, "content": "Rick Saez (54:04): Well, there's a fair amount of analytics on that. They blow right through the one on the front side, the intro roll. They don't even, because getting to the meat and potatoes, the mid roll one or two have the best chance of getting hurt all the way through, and they don't stick around to the end of the thing. They hear the end roll. So typically mid-roll is where it's at.\n\nCole Heilborn (54:22): Do you feel like podcasts, I feel like one of the reasons why podcasts are so great right now, another reason I guess is because there's limited advertising. They haven't blown up popular as popular of a place to advertise. Part of me is excited for advertising to become more of an option and a more lucrative option, but part of me also is not excited about that because I just can imagine more ads, more interrupting, more attention seeking ads rather than value adding ads.", "token_estimate": 224, "speakers": ["Rick Saez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "54:04", "end_timestamp": "54:22", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "fd04eb03-e8a5-4cf2-8437-fb72844fa0c8", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 34, "content": "Rick Saez (54:57): Well, I think that puts pressure on the sponsor and the host because I don't want to do an ad. I don't want to do just a read of what you're doing because again, that's interrupting the flow of our conversation. And as a host, I don't want to do that, but I am inclined to with Four wheel campers. The mid-roll ads are about the camper. It's more of a continuation of the podcast. It's a transition from talking to the guest to a read about, he was talking about his four wheel camper. Well, four wheel campers are made by blah, whatever it is, and it's a different one in each four episodes. So I think it's a way to continue that. But add value, you always want to add value, in my opinion, on whatever you're doing on your podcast. And so if you do it right and you do the ad read write, you don't make it too long. (55:47): You can't make it another two minutes. It's got to be maybe 30 seconds to a minute and a half max. They want to get back to the story, but it adds value to them so that as they're listening to me talk with Jonathan Seagrass about his four wheel camper, then you hear the guy from Four Wheel Camper or me or Jonathan, come on and talk about some more specifics about his four wheel camper, you're probably pretty well guaranteed. They're going to go home and look up four wheel campers and check it out. And that's ideally what you want to do. And that's I think the beauty of a podcast is you can go the route of radio and just slap some ads in there, but I think people will blow right through it. But if you have something where you're, again, continuing to provide value in education, they'll listen.\n\nCole Heilborn (56:27): Yeah. Well that's exciting. I'm excited to see how this format works for you, essentially making the content the ad, but making it valuable and making it entertaining and informative. So Rick, you also host an addition to creating a podcast and having a day job. You also host workshops that help folks with a variety of different things. We've kind of alluded to it. Could you give us a brief rundown of what you do?", "token_estimate": 523, "speakers": ["Rick Saez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "54:57", "end_timestamp": "56:27", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "48ce506a-c121-4c6f-981d-d14ff49edff3", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 35, "content": "Rick Saez (56:51): Yeah, so I have a couple of ways that you can, if you want to launch a podcast, I have a couple of ways you can do that. I have a course that you can, can't download it at the moment. We're having some issues with Stripe on my website. But you can go to my website, rick saes.com, and there are three links. One is listen, if you click the listen link, that takes you to where my podcast is and the latest episodes right there, you can find all the other episodes and show notes if you go to the learn link, that takes you to my podcast education. And then I'm also still doing some product design consulting. I was a product manager for a number of brands, so I'm helping people on that front and also people that just want to create a backpack. I worked with a guy about six to nine months ago who's got this cool chess pack to hold some of your stuff while you're skiing. (57:36): So he brought that to market. So that kind of thing. And then the third one is look, and that's where all my photography is and coaching on the podcast front. You can download the book and you get a couple of follow-up calls. You can pay for the five week course and I'll hold your hand all the way through it. Yeah, there's a couple ways to interact there and you can listen to, if you listen to the No Ordinary Adventure Podcast by Unre Adventures, I helped those guys launch that. I also worked with Kristen Carpenter Verde connected me with the guys at Leadville, and they started Grit Guts and Determination. I helped them launch that. So there's a couple recent podcasts that you can listen to that learn from some of the stuff that I taught.\n\nCole Heilborn (58:18): And if you don't subscribe currently to the Outdoor Biz podcast, you should because every Tuesday there'll be a new episode,\n\nRick Saez (58:25): Get on it. And then on the website you can also subscribe to my newsletter. I have a newsletter that goes out every Tuesday as well.\n\nCole Heilborn (58:30): Well, Rick, appreciate you taking the time to chat. This has been a lot of fun. I appreciate the conversations. And who knows, maybe in a few months from now we will see one of these ideas pop up on Apple Podcasts.", "token_estimate": 548, "speakers": ["Rick Saez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "56:51", "end_timestamp": "58:30", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "a0aff1e7-3393-4600-933e-fa09ad9f9de9", "episode_id": "da9c8a9a-031d-4f65-a28b-f962b4711a49", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4876", "chunk_index": 36, "content": "Rick Saez (58:42): That'd be great. That'd be pretty cool. Yep. And we got to reverse the seats. You got to come on my show coming up.\n\nCole Heilborn (58:47): Alright, we'll make it happen.\n\nRick Saez (58:48): Right on. Sounds good. Thanks Cole.\n\nCole Heilborn (58:49): Thanks Rick. Take care.\n\nRick Saez (58:50): See you.\n\nCole Heilborn (58:53): Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learned something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 140, "speakers": ["Rick Saez", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "58:42", "end_timestamp": "58:53", "podcast_index": 79, "episode_name": "Ep 79: Rick Saez | The Podcast Opportunity for Brands | Outdoor Biz Podcast", "slug": "ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-79-rick-saez-the-podcast-opportunity-for-brands-outdoor-biz-podcast", "guest_name": "Rick Saez", "guest_title": "Outdoor Biz Podcast", "categories": [], "short_description": "Rick Saez is an industry veteran when it comes to podcasting. With over 300 episodes published of his Outdoor Biz Podcast, he's been in the game a long time. During that time, he's noticed that many brands have yet to fully harness the power of the podcast. In this episode, we discuss the future of the podcast platform, sponsorships, the opportunity for brands, and we even help you get started by jamming on some ideas for shows!", "podcast_length": "44:101"}, {"id": "5f6ebf43-9eb3-4630-9f6b-676d8c2e4d75", "episode_id": "9c1d6bb4-2ee3-4841-868f-6e45b7c3cbb3", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4829", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:00): Welcome to Brand Story. My name is Cole Hilborn and I'm going to be your host Today Brand story is all about the marketing professionals who are working in the industry today. The landscape is constantly changing and evolving, takes really great smart people to run a successful brand, and that's what this podcast is all about. It's all about the people behind the brands we interact with, the challenges they face, as well as their personal journey. So if you're interested in learning more about what it takes to run a top brand, I hope you'll love this podcast and find something educational about it. Enjoy today's episode. Welcome to Brand Story. My name is Cole Hoborne, and today I'm sitting down with Casey Lorenzen, the content manager from Fial, Raven Casey, how are you doing?\n\nCasey Lorenzen (00:41): I'm doing great, man. Thanks for having me.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:43): Awesome. Yeah, no problem. I've got to say there's two unique things about this episode. Number one, you're the first person out of Washington state to be on the show, and two, that also means you're the first person to do a interview over Google Hangout, so congrats.\n\nCasey Lorenzen (00:59): There we go. Yeah, something like that. Yeah, nice. Well, I'm glad to be the first.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:04): Yeah. So let's start off by telling us what you do. Tell us about Raven for those, the people out there who might not know.\n\nCasey Lorenzen (01:16): Yeah, so YR is a Swedish outdoor brand, started in 1960 in Sweden. Obviously we've operated here in the US for about 10 years now. Myself, I manage all of our content here in North America so that it's an ever-growing territory for us. So yeah, from my side, it's a pretty awesome job I produce and kind of manage all of our photo and video assets that are coming in. So yeah, always something new keeps me on my toes. But yeah, it's a brand that I've always regarded, so when the opportunity came up to work for 'em, I jumped at it and it's been a good ride so far.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:04): Yeah, and how long have you been with them?\n\nCasey Lorenzen (02:06): About a year and a half now, so not terribly long, but", "token_estimate": 543, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Casey Lorenzen"], "start_timestamp": "00:00", "end_timestamp": "02:06", "podcast_index": 7, "episode_name": "Ep 7: Casey Lorenzen | Moving Backwards in Life by Giving up Working for Yourself? | Fjällräven", "slug": "ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Casey shares a bit about the work he's doing at Fjällräven as well as how he ended up managing content for the Swedish Heritage brand.", "podcast_length": "44:29"}, {"id": "8bd51284-1f14-4759-a148-73b4a0a94e06", "episode_id": "9c1d6bb4-2ee3-4841-868f-6e45b7c3cbb3", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4829", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Cole Heilborn (02:09): Okay, nice. So you guys are opening up a new store, you were saying that's going to be in Denver?\n\nCasey Lorenzen (02:17): Yeah, so we're opening our Denver flagship this summer. Obviously with the COVID situation, it's kind of touch and go with a lot of those sort of operations. But yeah, everything's on track right now and it's going to be kind of our biggest and baddest store so far. So we're excited about it.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:34): Yeah, that's awesome. When will it be open?\n\nCasey Lorenzen (02:37): We're shooting for mid-June, so yeah, right in our backyard. Our North American headquarters is based in Lewisville, Colorado, so just outside of Boulder. So yeah, we're excited to have that just down the street.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:49): Sweet. Tell me a little bit about the brand. What is the brand? Who is the brand? How do you guys describe yourself?\n\nCasey Lorenzen (02:59): That's an interesting question. Yeah, I mean, like I said, it's a Swedish heritage brand, so there's kind of two sides to that. I think when you talk about the brand in Europe, it's a little bit different than how we talk about it here in North America, just because the brand, it is still kind of in its infancy here year, needless to say, the market is growing rapidly for us here, so business is doing really well. So yeah, I mean, when you talk to any suite, it seems like they've grown up with the brand, they know exactly what it is, what it stands for. When you talk to the typical American, I think the first question is how do you pronounce the name? So yeah, once you get past that, there's I guess this allure or intrigue associated with it because it is a foreign brand. But yeah, we focus and pride ourselves on durability, sustainability, and quality and longevity of our goods. So it's one of those things that when you purchase, you're going to have it for a long time. So that heritage backing is super important for us as we tell that story here in North America. So\n\nCole Heilborn (04:18): Yeah. So how do you do that? How do you tell that story here where there's a cultural difference and even as something as simple as just the name and how you pronounce it?", "token_estimate": 548, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Casey Lorenzen"], "start_timestamp": "02:09", "end_timestamp": "04:18", "podcast_index": 7, "episode_name": "Ep 7: Casey Lorenzen | Moving Backwards in Life by Giving up Working for Yourself? | Fjällräven", "slug": "ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Casey shares a bit about the work he's doing at Fjällräven as well as how he ended up managing content for the Swedish Heritage brand.", "podcast_length": "44:29"}, {"id": "95af0995-1edd-4f85-9b1b-08e2117acdb5", "episode_id": "9c1d6bb4-2ee3-4841-868f-6e45b7c3cbb3", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4829", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Casey Lorenzen (04:27): Yeah, I mean, I think we try to use that to our advantage just because when somebody hears about it for the first time, obviously whenever you hear about a new brand, you always have that hesitancy of is this legit and all that? So it is nice because like I said before I started working for the brand, I always held it in high regard because I had owned the products personally and I knew that the quality was there and I really enjoyed using their product. And so when you speak to the brand to somebody that doesn't understand or know it, when you then say that it's been around since 1960 in Sweden, you have that kind of heritage backing and okay, this is a brand built for longevity and has been around for a while and kind of know what we're doing. So\n\nCole Heilborn (05:23): Yeah,\n\nCasey Lorenzen (05:25): It's easier to tell that story because of that.\n\nCole Heilborn (05:27): Sure. I guess in your experience do Americans have, when they think of a Swedish brand, what do they think of? Do they think of that legacy heritage or I guess, what's the most common kind of thoughts about what a Swedish brand is or should be?\n\nCasey Lorenzen (05:48): Yeah, I can just speak for myself and what my perceptions would be, which I think anything Scandinavian I think you immediately go to, you have to be built for it. I mean, it's a hardy people, they endure harsh winters, and so when it comes to outdoor equipment and outdoor gear, I think they have that to fall back on and put their stamp on. But not only that, but as a Swedish brand, I think Swedish design is such a huge thing just across the board. And so I know from personal experience, but one of the main things that drew me to the brand in the first place was the fit of the garments, especially our trousers. That was one of the first pieces I had purchased from the brand, and it felt different to me than any other kind of outdoor pant that I had tried on to that point. And you kind of feel something when you put 'em on, if you will. It's just a little bit different. And I think that kind of goes back to that Swedish design. They put so much thought and effort into that.", "token_estimate": 542, "speakers": ["Casey Lorenzen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "04:27", "end_timestamp": "05:48", "podcast_index": 7, "episode_name": "Ep 7: Casey Lorenzen | Moving Backwards in Life by Giving up Working for Yourself? | Fjällräven", "slug": "ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Casey shares a bit about the work he's doing at Fjällräven as well as how he ended up managing content for the Swedish Heritage brand.", "podcast_length": "44:29"}, {"id": "f3384f3c-9047-40e7-b012-54276250e974", "episode_id": "9c1d6bb4-2ee3-4841-868f-6e45b7c3cbb3", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4829", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Cole Heilborn (07:07): Yeah. What's your guys's mission? What are you doing?\n\nCasey Lorenzen (07:13): So our mission is to inspire people to walk with nature. Like I said, and this kind of goes back to one of your earlier questions, which is what are we about? If Theall Raven as a brand has never been one to be fastest, lightest, top of mountain kind of thing, our mission is to get people to get outside and enjoy themselves in whatever capacity that is. That's great if you're bagging peaks, but it's also great if you're walking in a park with your family, whatever that nature looks like to you, we want people to get out and enjoy\n\nCole Heilborn (07:50): It. If you had to describe what makes a strong brand, are there core principles that come to mind for you?\n\nCasey Lorenzen (08:01): For me, it's almost a personal or emotional connection with the brand. I think that's when a brand is at its strongest is when somebody thinks of a brand, when it's top of mind, and when they have something more than just a jacket or a shirt from them, there's an experience that went along with that, whether that's when they purchase, having a connection with the salespeople or going through some sort of experience that was memorable in that piece of gear. That I think resonates for me. That's kind of what sticks out in my mind. Or if you've had a good experience with customer service or something like that where you would actually go out of your way to tell somebody about or to write a review about, I think that's the biggest win from the brand side.\n\nCole Heilborn (08:54): Yeah, no, I think, and not to talk too much about just the current state of things, but it's interesting to see how brands are messaging and what they're doing during these COVID times. This is the perfect, I don't want to say it's a great time, but it's also, it kind of is to see how brands are living up to their values when people and brands are under stress that people's values show. And so it's been cool to see which brands follow up with what maybe their brand principles are and which ones maybe do something different.", "token_estimate": 528, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Casey Lorenzen"], "start_timestamp": "07:07", "end_timestamp": "08:54", "podcast_index": 7, "episode_name": "Ep 7: Casey Lorenzen | Moving Backwards in Life by Giving up Working for Yourself? | Fjällräven", "slug": "ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Casey shares a bit about the work he's doing at Fjällräven as well as how he ended up managing content for the Swedish Heritage brand.", "podcast_length": "44:29"}, {"id": "0d6102e3-59b5-41de-939f-f63c46e50191", "episode_id": "9c1d6bb4-2ee3-4841-868f-6e45b7c3cbb3", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4829", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Casey Lorenzen (09:31): Yeah, certainly it's been interesting even just outside of the outdoor industry, seeing what the cadence has been with other brands, and I think you see really cool examples of what brands do. And then also I think you kind of hear crickets from others where you'd, otherwise, for me, I would've thought I would've heard something from some of these people. But yeah, definitely it changes things for\n\nCole Heilborn (09:57): Sure. Yeah. So one of the things you mentioned when we were chatting is that you guys took a lot of your content creation in house because that's been a hot topic amongst creatives. Do companies, do they bring stuff? Do they continue to outsource? How'd you guys make that decision and what does that look like for you guys?\n\nCasey Lorenzen (10:22): Yeah, I mean, that decision was, it was ultimately something that came down to sales for us. And like I said, in North America, our business is growing so rapidly we've kind of quickly become one of the global leaders for the brand. And so for us, like I said, the brand is so vastly different in how we communicate it in North America than we do in Sweden. And so we've kind of had to flip that on its head in terms of instead of receiving all of our assets from Sweden and then just kind of filling in the gaps, we are now trying to, I guess, drive a little bit more of that from our side so that we do speak to our consumer the right way with the right tone and with the right imagery and all of that, it is so important to do. And so yeah, I mean that shift has really come on in the last year and a half, maybe two years, where we're starting to drive that more, which is exciting too as a creative. It's really awesome to be at the forefront of that and kind of help build that out and shape what that looks like. So\n\nCole Heilborn (11:36): Did you find that when you were working with outside partners that they didn't quite get what you were going for and you felt like it was kind of a communicating the brand was a challenge to outside vendors? Is that you're saying?", "token_estimate": 519, "speakers": ["Casey Lorenzen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "09:31", "end_timestamp": "11:36", "podcast_index": 7, "episode_name": "Ep 7: Casey Lorenzen | Moving Backwards in Life by Giving up Working for Yourself? | Fjällräven", "slug": "ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Casey shares a bit about the work he's doing at Fjällräven as well as how he ended up managing content for the Swedish Heritage brand.", "podcast_length": "44:29"}, {"id": "68d37d3f-a182-46f0-b8e2-c5962e851fc3", "episode_id": "9c1d6bb4-2ee3-4841-868f-6e45b7c3cbb3", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4829", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Casey Lorenzen (11:49): Yeah, and that's something, I don't think that will ever go away completely, but it's definitely one of those things where anyone that we work with that is creating anything for the brand that would be consumer facing, there's an onboarding process, if you will. And it's definitely, it takes kind of a mentality shift, I think, for some people, because I think the way that we just approach things as Americans is very different in how we communicate to consumers just traditionally just I would say just across the board. And so I trying to funnel that into what the Swedish mentality is and bringing more of that humility into it. It can be challenging for sure, but it's also one of those things that I think a lot of people are intrigued by and interested in. And so we do get a lot of outreach from that side.\n\nCole Heilborn (12:49): Yeah, interesting. What are some campaigns that you guys have recently finished? And I guess what was the goal going into it, and what was the outcome?", "token_estimate": 254, "speakers": ["Casey Lorenzen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "11:49", "end_timestamp": "12:49", "podcast_index": 7, "episode_name": "Ep 7: Casey Lorenzen | Moving Backwards in Life by Giving up Working for Yourself? | Fjällräven", "slug": "ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Casey shares a bit about the work he's doing at Fjällräven as well as how he ended up managing content for the Swedish Heritage brand.", "podcast_length": "44:29"}, {"id": "d4aa51d7-fd59-405e-bedb-1f98ab734e57", "episode_id": "9c1d6bb4-2ee3-4841-868f-6e45b7c3cbb3", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4829", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Casey Lorenzen (12:59): I'd say one of the most recent that you'll see assets for right now currently is our spring summer campaign. I mean, I think just innately the situation has changed everything with that. So going into it, the whole idea behind it was to create our imagery that was a little bit different than what it had been in the past and kind of make our images and our emotion a little bit more emotive and kind of bring people into the frame rather than a viewer and bring them in as more of a participant. And so we did our photo shoot in New Zealand on the south island for spring summer 20 this year, and we worked with a photographer named Louise Whitehouse, who's a Swedish national who just has a really keen eye for natural light and just very natural situations. She's very good at just creating that rather than it being kind of an onset kind of thing. So (14:06): Yeah, we kind of took a step out just to work with somebody that we hadn't in the past that had the vision that we were looking to execute. And so when we went into that campaign, we were building assets for our 33 stores in North America. Everyone is different, and we've got light boxes to fill and POP and all this stuff. So our rundown for that was with that as the main goal in mind, and for the last, I'd say six weeks or whatever, those stores have been shut down. So all of those assets that we've created for those stores are not being shown in that way. And so we've really had to pivot how we're using those. And our team, we're small but mighty, and I'm super proud of what we've done to change our focus and redistribute those assets in a more digital sense. So\n\nCole Heilborn (15:03): Yeah,\n\nCasey Lorenzen (15:03): That's probably the most recent one that we're still working through, so\n\nCole Heilborn (15:08): Sure. Yeah, that's not something that I think a lot of people plan for. Many campaigns look like they've been redirected and having to adapt to meet the current times.\n\nCasey Lorenzen (15:19): Yeah, certainly.\n\nCole Heilborn (15:20): So tell me about your background. You've got quite the roller coaster\n\nCasey Lorenzen (15:25): That's", "token_estimate": 538, "speakers": ["Casey Lorenzen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "12:59", "end_timestamp": "15:25", "podcast_index": 7, "episode_name": "Ep 7: Casey Lorenzen | Moving Backwards in Life by Giving up Working for Yourself? | Fjällräven", "slug": "ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Casey shares a bit about the work he's doing at Fjällräven as well as how he ended up managing content for the Swedish Heritage brand.", "podcast_length": "44:29"}, {"id": "b37e3617-7b7d-4397-9ece-0140df8e3067", "episode_id": "9c1d6bb4-2ee3-4841-868f-6e45b7c3cbb3", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4829", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Cole Heilborn (15:26): Ended you up where you are. Let's dive into that.\n\nCasey Lorenzen (15:29): Yeah, certainly. I mean, I say, yeah, out of college, I kind of got my start in professional sports and live music. I'd say, yeah, a few years after that I started on the brand side and worked for Quicksilver for I'd say, I think seven years. And then, yeah, the last couple years I was there, me and a buddy started a backpack company. And so we owned and operated actually two backpack brands over the course of about eight years. And so yeah, it was one of those things. Our first brand was a pure outdoor brand. We had anything from 18 liter packs to 80 liter packs about as technical as you could come. And so yeah, we had a lot of success and some fun with that. We had some international distribution and sold into REI and a lot of good things. And so then the second pack brand was a lifestyle brand that we were one of the first to integrate smart technology into our bags. And so yeah, we found ourselves on Shark Tank through that venture, and then ultimately two and a half years ago, we sold those brands. So yeah, that's kind of where I found myself at Raven and doing what I'm doing now. But yeah, I mean, definitely an experience. I wouldn't trade for the world. I think it taught me every side of business whether I wanted to learn it or not, but it was definitely\n\nCole Heilborn (16:59): Fun. So let me clarify a little bit. What does smart technology in a backpack look like? Because most people wear a backpack, they want to get off the grid away from technology.\n\nCasey Lorenzen (17:12): Yeah, I mean, it was one of those things where we had a distributor in Japan that we were working with for our outdoor brand, and we were on a train in Tokyo, and both it kind of just hit us at the same time. We looked up and literally every person on the train was on their cell phone and had a backpack or a bag of some kind. And so we started just to formulate how we could integrate the two things.\n\nCole Heilborn (17:42): And", "token_estimate": 510, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Casey Lorenzen"], "start_timestamp": "15:26", "end_timestamp": "17:42", "podcast_index": 7, "episode_name": "Ep 7: Casey Lorenzen | Moving Backwards in Life by Giving up Working for Yourself? | Fjällräven", "slug": "ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Casey shares a bit about the work he's doing at Fjällräven as well as how he ended up managing content for the Swedish Heritage brand.", "podcast_length": "44:29"}, {"id": "aec6ada3-cd27-4e5a-a864-00aa43b49859", "episode_id": "9c1d6bb4-2ee3-4841-868f-6e45b7c3cbb3", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4829", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Casey Lorenzen (17:42): So yeah, that's kind of where it came from. And we ultimately landed on using smart in our packs that would charge your devices, but also were kind of out of the way, but integrated into the pack in a way that just seemed natural for you to be able to keep up with your daily lives. And then we also used wireless hard drives. Were kind of the upgraded version of that, so I guess for more of the techie person that needed that storage and everything on the go. So yeah, we were, I think, at the forefront of that, and that was kind of the shtick that got us onto Shark Tank and some notoriety in that regard.\n\nCole Heilborn (18:24): Yeah. Well, tell us about Shark Tank. Is it what it seems or what's it like\n\nCasey Lorenzen (18:30): It is? Yeah, I mean, talk about a roller coaster. That was definitely a roller coaster. I think we experienced every emotion under the sun going through that process. But yeah, no, it was amazing. And I would say going into it, I hadn't watched a lot of Shark Tank. I knew what the show was, but didn't really pay attention to it. And a buddy of ours that we shared an office with came into work one morning and told us that he had heard on the radio that they were doing open casting calls in Denver, and we were kind of at a point where we were looking to get it more off of the ground and get some exposure for it. So we ran down there. I think we were the second to last people to audition. And\n\nCole Heilborn (19:12): You didn't have anything planned, or", "token_estimate": 380, "speakers": ["Casey Lorenzen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "17:42", "end_timestamp": "19:12", "podcast_index": 7, "episode_name": "Ep 7: Casey Lorenzen | Moving Backwards in Life by Giving up Working for Yourself? | Fjällräven", "slug": "ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Casey shares a bit about the work he's doing at Fjällräven as well as how he ended up managing content for the Swedish Heritage brand.", "podcast_length": "44:29"}, {"id": "5094c68f-e891-491d-81eb-077a790bf0a3", "episode_id": "9c1d6bb4-2ee3-4841-868f-6e45b7c3cbb3", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4829", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Casey Lorenzen (19:14): No, I mean, we filled out, there was a, I don't know, a 20 page application or something and we busted as just filling that thing out and then got down there. Yeah, I mean, went through the whole process. I think there was five or six rounds of cuts basically in the season that we filmed for. There were 55,000 businesses that applied and they filmed 200 of them, and I think 150 got an episode or something. So even just getting on the show was a feat in itself, I'd say. But no, I will say mean to the show's credit. I mean, when we got there and got out on that stage, it was a full on investor meeting. I mean, there's no kind of retakes or, okay, no, you move over here, stand there, say this. None of that stuff when you get out there, I mean the sharks are all talking over each other and it's full on. But no, it was an amazing experience. I mean, we had all the heavy hitters there, and so we had some good banter with Cuban and some good back and forth. But yeah, ultimately it was an amazing experience.\n\nCole Heilborn (20:20): What did you learn going through that?\n\nCasey Lorenzen (20:23): I think I learned that I could probably do, I guess I could get in any situation and talk about my business or what I've been doing. I think when you are in such a high pressure situation like that, I don't know, I think it makes you grow as a person. So I think, yeah, that was probably the biggest takeaway. But\n\nCole Heilborn (20:46): Just in having some outside validation that you could represent your brand well, and I dunno, stand up to some of the big guys and share your", "token_estimate": 402, "speakers": ["Casey Lorenzen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "19:14", "end_timestamp": "20:46", "podcast_index": 7, "episode_name": "Ep 7: Casey Lorenzen | Moving Backwards in Life by Giving up Working for Yourself? | Fjällräven", "slug": "ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Casey shares a bit about the work he's doing at Fjällräven as well as how he ended up managing content for the Swedish Heritage brand.", "podcast_length": "44:29"}, {"id": "89e2d0a5-f77f-4be8-8ec0-bc23e385e6e0", "episode_id": "9c1d6bb4-2ee3-4841-868f-6e45b7c3cbb3", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4829", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Casey Lorenzen (20:58): Projects. Yeah, I think so. Yeah, I think so. And I mean, it was one of those things where when we walked out of there before we left Damon, John had kind of went on this tangent with us and was basically telling us that we were the next wave of entrepreneur. I mean, we were a couple of young guys at that point and just doing what we wanted to do and how he could kind of see us in his shoes one day, which was for, I mean, for a couple of mid 20-year-old guys trying to build backpacks, we were kind of like, okay, this pretty rad. So yeah, I think being put in that situation, I think innately just, it forces you to grow a little bit. And so that was an amazing experience.\n\nCole Heilborn (21:40): Yeah. So as an entrepreneur, well, okay, let me back up. Can you be an entrepreneur if you don't have your own business? Does that make sense?\n\nCasey Lorenzen (21:54): Yeah. And certainly I don't think I'll ever lose that side of me no matter who I'm working for or in what role. And honestly, I think that's what ultimately has brought me to where I am today, is because I did have exposure and experience in so many different facets of business, and especially in what I was concentrating on, which was more the marketing side. So I think it's one of those things that it's hard to put on paper, but when you are working with teams and you've got a lot of things happening, a lot of projects, and I think it's an invaluable thing for somebody to have that even just that drive and that spirit to be kind of more outward thinking and rather than just the cog in the machine. So that I think is, I'll always hold that in high regard is something that as I'm looking for other candidates and stuff, that's something I look for in people\n\nCole Heilborn (23:01): Because the whole idea of entrepreneurship running your own business is, I don't want to say it's like a fad, but it's kind of sexy. It's glorified a little bit for someone that's gone through it and done it and I think had success for whatever success looks like, however society would define it. What would you say, I guess, to that concept?", "token_estimate": 533, "speakers": ["Casey Lorenzen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "20:58", "end_timestamp": "23:01", "podcast_index": 7, "episode_name": "Ep 7: Casey Lorenzen | Moving Backwards in Life by Giving up Working for Yourself? | Fjällräven", "slug": "ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Casey shares a bit about the work he's doing at Fjällräven as well as how he ended up managing content for the Swedish Heritage brand.", "podcast_length": "44:29"}, {"id": "a881a986-8544-4c4c-bd0c-cffb1348062c", "episode_id": "9c1d6bb4-2ee3-4841-868f-6e45b7c3cbb3", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4829", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Casey Lorenzen (23:27): I would agree with that for sure. It definitely is one of those things where, I mean, even going through that, when people ask you what you did and when you said in any capacity, when you say, oh, I own my own business, that could be a Pinterest site or whatever. It varies in so many levels. I think there's people automatically kind of perk their ears up, like, oh, what do you do? Then there's this intrigue of what is it that you do? What is this business that you own? But on the flip side of that, there's a lot of dark days too. There's a lot of working on weekends and working super late and missing stuff that you wouldn't otherwise have missed if you're doing a nine to five kind of thing. But I think ultimately, if you're doing something that you're passionate about, then that doesn't matter as much. And after doing that for eight years, you definitely get beat down. But at the same time, if you keep that passion for it, it's something that you can keep going for as long as you need.\n\nCole Heilborn (24:27): Yeah, yeah. Interesting. I think one of the things that I've noticed, and I'm curious what you think, it's not necessarily the work that you take home on the weekends or after 5:00 PM but it's the constant pressure that you're not good enough or you don't know what you're doing. I don't know. Did you experience that?", "token_estimate": 342, "speakers": ["Casey Lorenzen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "23:27", "end_timestamp": "24:27", "podcast_index": 7, "episode_name": "Ep 7: Casey Lorenzen | Moving Backwards in Life by Giving up Working for Yourself? | Fjällräven", "slug": "ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Casey shares a bit about the work he's doing at Fjällräven as well as how he ended up managing content for the Swedish Heritage brand.", "podcast_length": "44:29"}, {"id": "0f36cadf-6e86-40a1-b976-1f06a89a6747", "episode_id": "9c1d6bb4-2ee3-4841-868f-6e45b7c3cbb3", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4829", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Casey Lorenzen (24:47): Not so much, but I definitely did. I think it changes your mindset in how you operate because it does ultimately fall on you, the success or the failure, you're not guaranteed that paycheck every two weeks. And so, I mean, I think the drive is, I wouldn't say it forces you, but it definitely holds your hand in that, that you've got to make something happen. And if you're not putting in the time or the effort, that's not going to work. So yeah, I mean, like I said, there's definitely the ups and downs. I think going through that, I developed some slight insomnia from that that hasn't quite totally gone away. I mean, it's just one of those things where the lights are always on and you're always just kind of thinking about what if this and what is that? And so yeah, there's that side to it too that nobody really sees.\n\nCole Heilborn (25:44): Yeah. What's it like being an entrepreneur in an industry, the outdoor industry, which it's always, it seems like it's all about relaxing, getting away from the stress of life. You're selling a lifestyle, yet your lifestyle is the opposite of that. What's that?\n\nCasey Lorenzen (26:08): It's interesting. Yeah. And I mean, my situation could be a hell of a lot worse if I was going from an entrepreneur in the outdoor industry to a desk job at a white collar firm or something like that. So I think the industry and the sector that I've found myself in is something that I probably won't ever leave, just because after being in it for so long and knowing how it operates, I don't think I could do anything else. But yeah, it's one of those things that I don't know, it's definitely different having a whole team of people that you're working with and answering to and figuring stuff out with, which is, I mean, there's pros and cons of that because definitely nice being able to look across the room and say, Hey, should we do this today? And you both decide to do it, so you do it rather than the slower trig of getting approvals on stuff.\n\nCole Heilborn (27:10): Sure.", "token_estimate": 512, "speakers": ["Casey Lorenzen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "24:47", "end_timestamp": "27:10", "podcast_index": 7, "episode_name": "Ep 7: Casey Lorenzen | Moving Backwards in Life by Giving up Working for Yourself? | Fjällräven", "slug": "ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Casey shares a bit about the work he's doing at Fjällräven as well as how he ended up managing content for the Swedish Heritage brand.", "podcast_length": "44:29"}, {"id": "b72a430e-beaf-4f7c-abcf-7e69b41364e7", "episode_id": "9c1d6bb4-2ee3-4841-868f-6e45b7c3cbb3", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4829", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Casey Lorenzen (27:11): Yeah. No, I mean, as far as the lifestyle goes, the brand that I'm with, if y'all, Raven, were growing so rapidly, and so there's a lot going on for us. And so there is a lot of work, but I would much rather be in that situation than on the opposite side of the fence and something that was struggling. Yeah, I mean, it's nice though because the brand definitely allows us to get outside and enjoy nature, I think more than any other company would. So\n\nCole Heilborn (27:48): To some people it would look like, okay, you started this company, you sold it, and then you went to work for someone else. Does that seem like you went backwards?\n\nCasey Lorenzen (27:58): Not in my opinion, just because, I mean, yeah, we had our own brands. We were operating pretty successfully for a while, but we were still a small company. I mean, there was a lot of ups and downs, and we kind of had these peaks and valleys that we had gone through for so long that it was like we kind of needed a reset. And so ultimately, if I was to do it all over again, I would've done the same exact thing. Going to an international brand with so much credibility, and I guess positive momentum was kind of the ultimate place for me to land in the moment. And I mean, that's the other thing too, is I'd never really been internal with a large brand in this capacity to this point. And so I've, even in a year and a half that I've been with the brand, I've learned so much on the other side of things than what I had as an entrepreneur.\n\nCole Heilborn (28:58): Sure. Yeah. Interesting. What gives you purpose? What's your drive? Where does that come from?", "token_estimate": 413, "speakers": ["Casey Lorenzen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "27:11", "end_timestamp": "28:58", "podcast_index": 7, "episode_name": "Ep 7: Casey Lorenzen | Moving Backwards in Life by Giving up Working for Yourself? | Fjällräven", "slug": "ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Casey shares a bit about the work he's doing at Fjällräven as well as how he ended up managing content for the Swedish Heritage brand.", "podcast_length": "44:29"}, {"id": "a8593d8a-46e3-4313-be78-81496c61b5e6", "episode_id": "9c1d6bb4-2ee3-4841-868f-6e45b7c3cbb3", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4829", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Casey Lorenzen (29:07): I think my purpose is to create, I love to not do what I'm supposed to do or what is expected of me. If you'll, I've always had a rebel mentality, and even back into grade school, one example I always gave is I never learned the dance, the macare, because I was in detention. It's awful. Detention. No, it was awful. And I was in detention at the time when everyone else was learning it. So anytime anyone does that dance, I'm like, nah, I don't know that because I was a terrible kid growing up, honestly. And I think that's a lot of what speaks to an entrepreneur is kind of questioning things and doing things a little bit differently. And so I think for me, I think one of the greatest pleasures I get is when I do something like that, when I kind of step outside what the norm is or what is expected, and I produce results that are better, that to me is I love that. And so that's, I guess that's a weird thing to see is my drive. But no, and I think just enjoying myself and what I'm doing and working with awesome people and having new experiences. So working for an outdoor brand that takes me all over the world and has me creating stuff is I'm kind of in a really good spot. So\n\nCole Heilborn (30:37): Yeah. What do you want to see change in the outdoor world, the industry?\n\nCasey Lorenzen (30:43): There's a lot of things. I mean, I think the mentality, I think there are some areas where it's gotten better, but I think there's still so much old school mentality of how things should be and how things should operate. And also I think transparency for certain, I would say brands or just how things are perceived, just, I don't know, you kind of see the whole thing. I mean, it's a small industry. You get to know a lot of people. And I mean, I don't know, teach his own, I guess I don't try to dive too much into what other people are doing. Just,\n\nCole Heilborn (31:29): I", "token_estimate": 478, "speakers": ["Casey Lorenzen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "29:07", "end_timestamp": "31:29", "podcast_index": 7, "episode_name": "Ep 7: Casey Lorenzen | Moving Backwards in Life by Giving up Working for Yourself? | Fjällräven", "slug": "ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Casey shares a bit about the work he's doing at Fjällräven as well as how he ended up managing content for the Swedish Heritage brand.", "podcast_length": "44:29"}, {"id": "715db7cd-44ae-4c9f-8267-a608b04c9540", "episode_id": "9c1d6bb4-2ee3-4841-868f-6e45b7c3cbb3", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4829", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Casey Lorenzen (31:30): Don't know. I kind of came into the industry more from the action sports background. And so there is kind of a different mentality for me either side. And I mean, it's still, I can't complain because it's still lifestyle. It's still in a really fun industry. It's not like we're selling printers or something like that.\n\nCole Heilborn (31:54): It's funny you mentioned that. I was listening to this book by this author called Marty, I dunno if you've heard of him, but he's this brand specialist guy, and someone asked him this question. It was like a live q and a, and he was like, they're talking about branding and what makes a good brand. And someone asked, well, how would you make a copier brand exciting? Because I dunno about you, but I think about that. I'm like, what would you even work with? But he, honestly, he had a really interesting response and it made me think a little deeper. And he was like, it might seem on the outside that it's like a plain dry, you can't do anything new. But he said, honestly, you're not being creative. You got to take off those blinders and look at something as stale as a copier and outside the box. I mean, what was that purple mattress or purple pillow? They looked at a pillow and they're like, we could make this way cooler. So it is just interesting listening to experts and their opinion on something that I would look at and be like, oh, you can't change that. Yeah. Right. Yeah,\n\nCasey Lorenzen (32:59): No, I think there's always opportunity for anything like that in really any industry. And I think it kind of goes back to what I was saying as far as just stepping outside of the norm and just doing something different or even just having a different approach at it and looking at it through a different set of glasses. So that to me, yeah, I hope I would never find myself in that situation where I'd have to try and make a copier exciting, but I think I could maybe come up with some ways.\n\nCole Heilborn (33:29): Cool, man. Well, we're about out of time, but I got one more question for you. What's been your most crazy outdoor adventure? If you could tell one story at a campfire, what would it be?", "token_estimate": 547, "speakers": ["Casey Lorenzen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "31:30", "end_timestamp": "33:29", "podcast_index": 7, "episode_name": "Ep 7: Casey Lorenzen | Moving Backwards in Life by Giving up Working for Yourself? | Fjällräven", "slug": "ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Casey shares a bit about the work he's doing at Fjällräven as well as how he ended up managing content for the Swedish Heritage brand.", "podcast_length": "44:29"}, {"id": "bee57b87-f50f-4f10-a966-ed29ec4fe690", "episode_id": "9c1d6bb4-2ee3-4841-868f-6e45b7c3cbb3", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4829", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Casey Lorenzen (33:46): I had a pretty cool one recently. I was fortunate enough to join the global team on one of their photo shoots in a Bisco Sweden, so the northern most part of Sweden, which is just in the Arctic Circle. And so it was dead of winter. I mean, I think temps got down to negative 10 or something. And so we were shooting a lot of motion for the project. And so we covered, there's snow everywhere, but we needed a snowstorm and we didn't have wind for the snowstorm. And so we had a leaf blower that was the worst leaf blower in the world. It kept malfunctioning on us and we couldn't get our snow to blow the way we needed it. And so we've got this whole team, I mean, everyone's there. We've got a team of 20 people there. And so we had this night scene in this tent where it's supposed to be this crazy rockus scene, and the models are setting up the tent in this big snowstorm. (34:49): And so we had to figure out a way to create a snowstorm out of that. And so we had a couple of snowmobiles, so we backed those up and we used the exhaust from the snowmobiles while five of us are shoveling snow into the tent area into the scene to create this havoc. And another person's shaking the tent to make the wind. And it was just one of those things where you kind of step back and you're like, what is happening right now? But then you see the footage and it looks like an amazing snowstorm. And so that was a recent one. I mean, there's a bunch, but I don't know. I feel like they always kind of come back to work. I think with my other brand, our factory was in Vietnam, and on one of the weekends we went for a hike with some of our factory workers and they're like, oh, it's going to be this easy hike. It's whatever. And so we show up in vans and shorts and just like whatever. And we ended up hiking and it was like 13 miles round trip up a volcano. So we're at one point grabbing tree roots to get up the side of this mountain, and I'm in my skate shoes, but that was another fun one just because what are we doing?\n\nCole Heilborn (36:08): Yeah,\n\nCasey Lorenzen (36:10): There's a lot of 'em, bud.", "token_estimate": 531, "speakers": ["Casey Lorenzen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "33:46", "end_timestamp": "36:10", "podcast_index": 7, "episode_name": "Ep 7: Casey Lorenzen | Moving Backwards in Life by Giving up Working for Yourself? | Fjällräven", "slug": "ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Casey shares a bit about the work he's doing at Fjällräven as well as how he ended up managing content for the Swedish Heritage brand.", "podcast_length": "44:29"}, {"id": "b9821426-5ac8-48d1-b4a9-10b04972870f", "episode_id": "9c1d6bb4-2ee3-4841-868f-6e45b7c3cbb3", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4829", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Cole Heilborn (36:11): Cool, man. Well, I appreciate you taking the time to chat. It's been great to learn a little bit more about your story. If people want to follow along with your adventures, where can they find you?\n\nCasey Lorenzen (36:23): I guess just on Instagram, I mean at KC Lorenzen, but also p Raven Na is kind of where all the professional stuff lies, so yeah, that's probably the best place.\n\nCole Heilborn (36:35): Cool, man. Well, thank you again. I really appreciate it, and I'll let you get back to your day.\n\nCasey Lorenzen (36:41): Hey, I appreciate it, man. Thank you so much for having me on.\n\nCole Heilborn (36:43): Yeah, take care. Thank you for listening to Brand Story. If you enjoyed this episode, I encourage you to share it with a friend or family member who also might enjoy, and please tune in next time for the next episode. Thank you.", "token_estimate": 217, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Casey Lorenzen"], "start_timestamp": "36:11", "end_timestamp": "36:43", "podcast_index": 7, "episode_name": "Ep 7: Casey Lorenzen | Moving Backwards in Life by Giving up Working for Yourself? | Fjällräven", "slug": "ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-7-casey-lorenzen-moving-backwards-in-life-by-giving-up-working-for-yourself-fjallraven", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this episode, Casey shares a bit about the work he's doing at Fjällräven as well as how he ended up managing content for the Swedish Heritage brand.", "podcast_length": "44:29"}, {"id": "de81a556-9ceb-4735-917e-8769d700c7b7", "episode_id": "8d72a24e-5891-43be-b320-ead7f102ad1d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4878", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Darren Kinnaird (00:04): One of the most important things that I picked up from him was make sure you are telling your stakeholders, your sponsors, all the scenarios, and work them through and have them involved in all of the scenarios that you're planning. And so that was one of the most critical things that I think we're able to do is have weekly or biweekly calls with all of our partners, all of our stakeholders, just to keep them up to date on what we're doing, what we're thinking.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:33): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hilborn and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Back Country Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Portside Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. Today I'm sitting down with Darren Kard. He's the managing director of the Crank Works World Tour. Darren, how are you doing?\n\nDarren Kinnaird (01:14): I am fantastic, Cole. Thank you for asking yourself.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:17): I'm great stoked to be chatting today. I am not in New Zealand right now, but that's where you find yourself, huh?\n\nDarren Kinnaird (01:26): Yes, day eight of my mandatory quarantine here in Auckland. There's been a bunch of the athletes in our team and staff fly down here and people are spread out all over the country. Really bizarre experience, but really thankful that we've been able to come to New Zealand under the current times. So yeah, just thankful to be here.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:54): What does quarantine look like? Are you confined to a hotel room or how does that work?", "token_estimate": 486, "speakers": ["Darren Kinnaird", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:04", "end_timestamp": "01:54", "podcast_index": 80, "episode_name": "Ep 80: Darren Kinnaird | Event Marketing, Maintaining Sponsorships and COVID | Crankworx", "slug": "ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "guest_name": "Darren Kinnaird", "guest_title": "Crankworx", "categories": [], "short_description": "Of all the industries to get hammered the most during COVID, the events industry has been hit hard. Forced to cancel, post-pone, shutter doors, and limit capacities. Events around the world have been struggling to operate with the restrictions.", "podcast_length": "52:50"}, {"id": "642aa2d0-b1ba-4726-a72a-ca74deed0707", "episode_id": "8d72a24e-5891-43be-b320-ead7f102ad1d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4878", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Darren Kinnaird (01:58): Yeah, so we're confined to the hotel room. They deliver three meals a day. Food's been pretty good. We get the opportunity to book walks where they've set up two walking loops, one sort of on a balcony and one down near the lobby. And you can go for 30 minute walks, they say once a day, but we've been able to sneak into sometimes three a day if it's not too busy. But yeah, exercise in the room. You can order groceries. They got Uber Eats here. A lot of the grocery stores deliver, so it's a strange, I wouldn't call it a vacation, but yeah, it's a strange, strange experience, but we've been able to get a lot of work done as we prepare for Crank Works, roto in a couple of weeks.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:52): How many days more days do you have?\n\nDarren Kinnaird (02:56): So it's a total of 14, so this is my last week. Today is Thursday here, Thursday morning, and we arrived one week ago, so another week to go.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:06): Wow. And so when you're not in New Zealand, where do you live? Where are you from?\n\nDarren Kinnaird (03:11): Well, home is Squamish, BC about 30 minutes south of Whistler. I moved to Whistler in March of 1999, and I've never looked back. I wasn't sure how long I would stay in Whistler, but when they announced the Olympics in 2003, it was sort of one of those. Okay. Yeah, I got to experience this will be a once in a lifetime experience and yeah, moved to Squamish in 2013 and I've called that home since.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:43): Well, I'm stoked to jump into our topic today. We're going to be kind of diving in deep talking about sponsorships, events and how COVID is affecting all of those things and how you and your team with the Crank Works are figuring out ways to make it work. We were introduced via Rob mc skimming, he was on the podcast episode 68. He kind of gave us a great backstory of how Crank Works was started. Could you kind of catch us up though, when did you first start interacting with the organization and what's your role now as a managing director?", "token_estimate": 510, "speakers": ["Darren Kinnaird", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "01:58", "end_timestamp": "03:43", "podcast_index": 80, "episode_name": "Ep 80: Darren Kinnaird | Event Marketing, Maintaining Sponsorships and COVID | Crankworx", "slug": "ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "guest_name": "Darren Kinnaird", "guest_title": "Crankworx", "categories": [], "short_description": "Of all the industries to get hammered the most during COVID, the events industry has been hit hard. Forced to cancel, post-pone, shutter doors, and limit capacities. Events around the world have been struggling to operate with the restrictions.", "podcast_length": "52:50"}, {"id": "48d8627f-01a3-487a-83c3-0d8f73399f58", "episode_id": "8d72a24e-5891-43be-b320-ead7f102ad1d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4878", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Darren Kinnaird (04:16): Yeah, so I actually started as a volunteer in 2005 with Crankworx. I did a couple of years just volunteering. I was also working for the Worcester Black Home Ski and Snowboard School in the winters, so I knew Rob back then. It was in around 2007, 2008 when a gentleman by the name of Jeremy Roche, who was the general manager of Crankworx and actually worked for Rob on business development, asked me to get involved doing some client service stuff looking after some of the partners. Did that for a few years, took over some of the sponsorship stuff for the bike park and for Crank Works. And then in September of 2010, sat down for a coffee with Rob and he had said that Jeremy was looking to take his career in a different direction and would I be interested in becoming the new GM of Crankworx? And I mean, I couldn't have thought of a better job or career. I've always been such a big, big sports fan growing up. And so to be involved in a sporting entity, a sports league now is kind of a dream come true. And I pinched myself regularly. I've been the GM since 2010. The role has evolved a little bit. I'm now considered the managing director, but it's essentially a similar position.\n\nCole Heilborn (06:07): Great. And what's a day-to-day look like? What do you find yourself doing?", "token_estimate": 329, "speakers": ["Darren Kinnaird", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "04:16", "end_timestamp": "06:07", "podcast_index": 80, "episode_name": "Ep 80: Darren Kinnaird | Event Marketing, Maintaining Sponsorships and COVID | Crankworx", "slug": "ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "guest_name": "Darren Kinnaird", "guest_title": "Crankworx", "categories": [], "short_description": "Of all the industries to get hammered the most during COVID, the events industry has been hit hard. Forced to cancel, post-pone, shutter doors, and limit capacities. Events around the world have been struggling to operate with the restrictions.", "podcast_length": "52:50"}, {"id": "9dc21af7-74bd-4abc-9b40-c905e65544e6", "episode_id": "8d72a24e-5891-43be-b320-ead7f102ad1d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4878", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Darren Kinnaird (06:12): Yeah, a lot of out of event time. It's a lot of planning and working with our team, our Crank Works Events, Inc team, which is the actual name of the company. And we have three directors, one that oversees operations, one that oversees sport and one that oversees marketing and communications. I still work really closely with the sponsorship side of things and with our sponsorship manager. And then we do have another sort of arm of the company that's sort of our broadcast production where we work with third party, the Boombox group. And so working with the team and making sure our cohesive vision of how and where we're taking the property is going, but then also working with our local organizing committees in Innsbrook here in Rota as well, and sort of knowledge transfer, keeping them up to speed on what the big picture plans and directions are. And at the same time looking for new business development opportunities, whether they're with Crankworx or helping other sport entities, resorts, destinations, et cetera, to create. I think what we've been able to create with Crankworx is these just lifetime, memorable money, can't buy experiences when you go to one of these festivals. That's just second to none, and trying to share that experience with as many places that we can.\n\nCole Heilborn (07:59): Yeah, awesome. For folks that maybe aren't familiar with Crankworx, could you kind of give us the highlights? What is Crankworx?", "token_estimate": 368, "speakers": ["Darren Kinnaird", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "06:12", "end_timestamp": "07:59", "podcast_index": 80, "episode_name": "Ep 80: Darren Kinnaird | Event Marketing, Maintaining Sponsorships and COVID | Crankworx", "slug": "ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "guest_name": "Darren Kinnaird", "guest_title": "Crankworx", "categories": [], "short_description": "Of all the industries to get hammered the most during COVID, the events industry has been hit hard. Forced to cancel, post-pone, shutter doors, and limit capacities. Events around the world have been struggling to operate with the restrictions.", "podcast_length": "52:50"}, {"id": "81659ec9-94b4-4eb3-ace1-f12663c1a06c", "episode_id": "8d72a24e-5891-43be-b320-ead7f102ad1d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4878", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Darren Kinnaird (08:06): Yeah, so like Robert said in his episode, I was born in 2004. It's a festival of all things gravity, mountain biking, downhill racing, slope style, speed and style, pump track, duals, solemn, Enduro, kids racing, everything. A lot of the events and disciplines that we do at Crank Works, we've kind of made up, I mean Slope Style was born a Whistler in the early two thousands and sort of precursors to Crank Works speed and style. I think Rob was explaining how that was born out of Kyle Strike goofing around on the slalom track, it's more than just the sport, it's the celebration of all the elements of mountain biking or gravity mountain biking. We have multimedia events, photography contests, video contests. It's just really the full culmination of the celebration of the culture and all based in gravity mountain biking.\n\nCole Heilborn (09:19): Beautiful. So let's dive in prep for our conversation today. I was just doing some Google searching and I typed and I think sponsor event sponsorships and COVID, and I got a lot of topics that popped up. A few of the headlines were how are sponsors responding to live events amongst COVID, how COVID has changed sponsorship marketing, how to get the most out of post pandemic event sponsorships. So there's a lot of stuff, a lot of material out there that folks could start to read up on if they're curious. How is this all shaking out? But I'd love to hear from you. Kind of take us back. What has the last year and a half looked like for Crankworx in the event business? And let's start there.", "token_estimate": 394, "speakers": ["Darren Kinnaird", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "08:06", "end_timestamp": "09:19", "podcast_index": 80, "episode_name": "Ep 80: Darren Kinnaird | Event Marketing, Maintaining Sponsorships and COVID | Crankworx", "slug": "ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "guest_name": "Darren Kinnaird", "guest_title": "Crankworx", "categories": [], "short_description": "Of all the industries to get hammered the most during COVID, the events industry has been hit hard. Forced to cancel, post-pone, shutter doors, and limit capacities. Events around the world have been struggling to operate with the restrictions.", "podcast_length": "52:50"}, {"id": "ad5ff3c6-428d-46cb-be1b-ad6d611ee6b4", "episode_id": "8d72a24e-5891-43be-b320-ead7f102ad1d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4878", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Darren Kinnaird (09:58): Sure. If I take us back to March of 2020, we had just finished Crankworx Rota here at the very beginning of March. And when I left Canada COVID was, the headline was a guest travels from Vancouver to Toronto test positive or something like that. So it wasn't a big deal and or at least it wasn't headlines yet. And when I landed in New Zealand, there was some awareness of it. There was a couple of countries where they were sort of making you isolate for two weeks at a hotel or your home or something if you were traveling from those countries. But it wasn't a big thing yet. When I returned home, whole other story, I think I went into the office for one day, we made the decision to send everybody home the next day and after that, just like the world shut down. And so we started talking with the team in Innsbruck. (11:16): Are we in a postponed situation like what's happening? And we really, I mean at first we were is Craig or Whistler even going to be able to happen this year? And we quickly realized within a week or two that this was going to be a long term issue thing. I mean, I think it's probably much longer than I would've anticipated, and the scope of this whole thing is bigger than I would've imagined. But one of the things that we did realize quickly was that where we lived, we were pretty blessed that we had, and I think I actually realized this when I was out for a mountain bike ride with my son one afternoon. And just like where we live in Squamish and in bc, sea to Sky, we have some of the best mountain bikers in the world from all disciplines. And I think that's just a result of the riding opportunities in the sea of sky. (12:20): The Western Mountain Bike Park has been a massive, massive cultural influence on this area or the area where I live. So we have a lot of the riders, we have some of the best bike parks and riding in bc. We have a lot of the industry based in bc Pink Bike is there, giant Canada, Norco. A lot of these iconic mountain bike brands have their offices or Canadian offices anyways in nbc. And so we thought if the rest of the world basically can't do anything, maybe we could pull something off with the advantages we have in nbc. And so one of the first people we spoke to about this was the folks at Destination BC or the Ministry of Tourism because they're big supporters of Crankworx traditionally, and the funding that would've been coming for Crankworx Whistler was announced two days after all this stuff, after everything went down and they were kind of like, ah, well here's the money, let's see what you can do with it. (13:38): And we had this idea of, okay, well what if we took Crankworx on a road trip and we showcased and highlighted other destinations around the province gave these BC opportunity or BC athletes an opportunity to shine most of whom are from BC or have moved to BC because of the opportunities. And they just thought that was an amazing idea. And from there we hit the ground running, we went to our partners of Crank horse, Whistler explained what it was we were trying to do, how it was going to be made for tv, digital first broadcast first, no one on site. And I think at the time a lot of people were, they weren't sure what they were going to do from a marketing perspective. And so we were sort of one of the first to come to a lot of them with this concept and this idea and the feedback was generally really good. (14:39): They were like, okay, we think this is a great idea. We want to support you guys because we want you to be around in the long term. And so they got on board in a big way. And so it allowed us to, I mean for us it was the ultimate pivot. It kept our whole team working. We had about 70, including the athletes, I think last year we had around 65, 70 people on the tour plus the people that were in the resorts. And it kept people doing stuff and it kept us top of mind. And more importantly, it kept our sponsors and partners out there associated with Crank Works. We took what we had learned from that, put it into the event in Innsbrook in the fall that year, kind of a little bit of different rules when we went there. But again, right after we left there, I think Austria went into its second lockdown. (15:40): So we got that event just in time. So since then it's been an uncertain roller coaster, but we sort of built out this new platform plan digital first that brands wanted to be a part of. Our partners believed in. Of course, everyone wants to get back to the big festivals with tens of thousands of people in attendance, but we've managed to create something of value for our partners and for the destinations and of course for all our athletes and other stakeholders. So it's been a lot of working with partners, figuring things out as we move forward, but we're pretty stoked that we've been able to get Crank s book done, crank BCN as an alternative for Whistle this year. And now Crankworx Rota in a couple of weeks here.", "token_estimate": 1248, "speakers": ["Darren Kinnaird"], "start_timestamp": "09:58", "end_timestamp": "09:58", "podcast_index": 80, "episode_name": "Ep 80: Darren Kinnaird | Event Marketing, Maintaining Sponsorships and COVID | Crankworx", "slug": "ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "guest_name": "Darren Kinnaird", "guest_title": "Crankworx", "categories": [], "short_description": "Of all the industries to get hammered the most during COVID, the events industry has been hit hard. Forced to cancel, post-pone, shutter doors, and limit capacities. Events around the world have been struggling to operate with the restrictions.", "podcast_length": "52:50"}, {"id": "3962c8ac-1758-4543-a96f-172262313c4f", "episode_id": "8d72a24e-5891-43be-b320-ead7f102ad1d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4878", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Cole Heilborn (16:43): So to back up everything, shut down. The events that you were planning, those got canceled or postponed?\n\nDarren Kinnaird (16:51): Yeah, so Whistler got canceled. I mean Whistler, the setup takes about from the first shovels in the ground is about four months. And I mean every year in Whistler we have to rebuild the slopestyle course, which is a great advantage. We get a new course every year, but it's also you have to rebuild it every year. And in May of 2020, there was no sign of any certainty whatsoever. And so the decision was to cancel Crank Corps Whistler in Innsbruck. They were able to identify alternate dates in early October. And so we were able to postpone sbr. And I mean it was a challenge because a lot of, I mean at the time the US wasn't allowed to travel anywhere, so none of the American athletes could really get to Europe. It was primarily European based athletes. I mean a lot of the North American athletes were just hunkered down, weren't really sure about traveling internationally. Anyways, we've learned a lot since then. But yeah, we did have to cancel Whistler because of that lead up. And just generally what was going on.\n\nCole Heilborn (18:17): So when you had to cancel Whistler, I mean I'm sure that was a giant decision that weighed on everyone's shoulders as you were making it. How did you think through that and how were you thinking about the future repercussions of potentially canceling it?", "token_estimate": 365, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Darren Kinnaird"], "start_timestamp": "16:43", "end_timestamp": "18:17", "podcast_index": 80, "episode_name": "Ep 80: Darren Kinnaird | Event Marketing, Maintaining Sponsorships and COVID | Crankworx", "slug": "ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "guest_name": "Darren Kinnaird", "guest_title": "Crankworx", "categories": [], "short_description": "Of all the industries to get hammered the most during COVID, the events industry has been hit hard. Forced to cancel, post-pone, shutter doors, and limit capacities. Events around the world have been struggling to operate with the restrictions.", "podcast_length": "52:50"}, {"id": "dabe78ff-4cb8-4320-bc00-4df7c3e3d633", "episode_id": "8d72a24e-5891-43be-b320-ead7f102ad1d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4878", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Darren Kinnaird (18:34): Well, it is funny because the way my brain works and it never stops is I had already gone in the direction of the Cliff Crankworx summer series as this pivot that only we could pull off. And so by the time it actually came time to announce Crankworx Whistler, it was almost more of a formality. I was just excited about this crazy pivot that we had come up with and was excited to show that to the world that it was something unique that we could pull off. Of course, we were the sadness and disappointment of not being able to do Whistler was we dealt with that really quickly, moved on, the whole team got excited about, here we're going, we're going to do something else. No one else in the world can do this. We're going to pull this off. And so I think the whole team bought into we're going to go in a different direction. (19:40): I mean, it was sad and tough to make that call on Whistler. It was probably tougher this year because so many people had thought this won't be a thing for more than 6, 8, 12 months, whatever the heck it is. And so when we were faced with the reality of a second year of not being able to do this, that was probably tough. Year one was like, okay, cool, we're going to do something fun and new and it'll be different. And then I think to answer the other part of this, what were the long-term or what is our view of the long-term ramifications of that? (20:27): From my perspective, it was super, super important to keep Crank works, going, doing something and continuing to be able to tell our key sports stories and to keep, for lack of a better word, the narrative going. And that's what we've been able to do by working really hard to come up with ways to deliver Crank Steinburg this June with we had 500 spectators on site Crank Works BC where it was all made for tv. And then of course now here we think we're going to have spectators on site, but we're not a hundred percent certain with that. But it was just really important for the long-term of Crank Works that we continue the stories, we continue to provide opportunities for the athletes, we continue to provide opportunities for our partners. They're different opportunities, but it is really important to just keep working with all of these stakeholders to keep things going.", "token_estimate": 576, "speakers": ["Darren Kinnaird"], "start_timestamp": "18:34", "end_timestamp": "18:34", "podcast_index": 80, "episode_name": "Ep 80: Darren Kinnaird | Event Marketing, Maintaining Sponsorships and COVID | Crankworx", "slug": "ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "guest_name": "Darren Kinnaird", "guest_title": "Crankworx", "categories": [], "short_description": "Of all the industries to get hammered the most during COVID, the events industry has been hit hard. Forced to cancel, post-pone, shutter doors, and limit capacities. Events around the world have been struggling to operate with the restrictions.", "podcast_length": "52:50"}, {"id": "eee75187-86c7-406d-b9fe-f6427cfe6bcc", "episode_id": "8d72a24e-5891-43be-b320-ead7f102ad1d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4878", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Cole Heilborn (21:39): So what was the summer series that you guys came up with? What did that look like?\n\nDarren Kinnaird (21:48): There was a lot of talk at the time about this creation of bubbles and the NBA did it and the NHL did it. And early on I think there was a tennis tournament that took place in Croatia. There were some surfing events where they were just bringing people together that were already in destinations, and that was effectively what summer series was. And like I said, excuse me, like I said earlier, we have some of the best World Cup downhill racers. We have some of the best EWS Enduro racers, and we have some of the best crankworx focused athletes that live in bc. And we started out really early on by getting all of these athletes on a phone call and saying, Hey guys, none of us are probably traveling this summer. We got an idea. What do you think? And the athletes, they were the ones that first said, and I was really hoping this was going to happen. (23:02): They said, everybody has to race everything and it's a winner, takes all points for everything. And so we set it up over three weeks in July and August of 2020, we traveled and we had a bus pick people up from the ferry and in Pemberton and brought everyone to Silver Star. One of the athletes had built a really great slalom track there. We had the athletes race, Enduro Air downhill, downhill and slalom at every stop, with the exception of when we went to Golden Kicking Horse, we reintroduced psychosis to the world, which is this infamous race that hadn't taken place in 12 or 13 years since 2008. But it was really this, we would go a week, one week in Silver Star, one week in kicking Horse Golden, and then a week in Sun Peaks Resort. And I mean, these are three of BC's best bike park slash mountain biking destinations. And so it was neat to be able to share those locations with the rest of the world, but we had 24 athletes compete over three weeks. And I mean, the feedback was, it was a unique once in a lifetime experience. It was a lot of fun, heck of a lot of work, but was well worth it,\n\nCole Heilborn (24:35): Man. I'm impressed with how quick you guys came up with something else.", "token_estimate": 550, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Darren Kinnaird"], "start_timestamp": "21:39", "end_timestamp": "24:35", "podcast_index": 80, "episode_name": "Ep 80: Darren Kinnaird | Event Marketing, Maintaining Sponsorships and COVID | Crankworx", "slug": "ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "guest_name": "Darren Kinnaird", "guest_title": "Crankworx", "categories": [], "short_description": "Of all the industries to get hammered the most during COVID, the events industry has been hit hard. Forced to cancel, post-pone, shutter doors, and limit capacities. Events around the world have been struggling to operate with the restrictions.", "podcast_length": "52:50"}, {"id": "384022d3-2c1f-4c02-9f22-df4a9da8b93d", "episode_id": "8d72a24e-5891-43be-b320-ead7f102ad1d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4878", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Darren Kinnaird (24:41): Thanks. Yeah, I mean, like I said, my brain never stops and I love sports. I love looking at what other sports do, what they did to Pivot, but also just what they do in general to make whatever it is they're doing special. So yeah, just the inspiration from all places.\n\nCole Heilborn (25:07): So as you're working through all these changes that are just kind of coming at you from left field or right field or anywhere and you're considering, how do we keep our sponsors and our partners in mind as we're trying to navigate all of this stuff? So typically Crank Works has a bunch of partners and sponsors that help put on the event. How do you work with them now and how are you trying to serve them and still provide value even though the thing that they usually sign up for is totally different?", "token_estimate": 206, "speakers": ["Darren Kinnaird", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "24:41", "end_timestamp": "25:07", "podcast_index": 80, "episode_name": "Ep 80: Darren Kinnaird | Event Marketing, Maintaining Sponsorships and COVID | Crankworx", "slug": "ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "guest_name": "Darren Kinnaird", "guest_title": "Crankworx", "categories": [], "short_description": "Of all the industries to get hammered the most during COVID, the events industry has been hit hard. Forced to cancel, post-pone, shutter doors, and limit capacities. Events around the world have been struggling to operate with the restrictions.", "podcast_length": "52:50"}, {"id": "cd2f1e58-7ed7-4569-8fe0-72a8efc14329", "episode_id": "8d72a24e-5891-43be-b320-ead7f102ad1d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4878", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Darren Kinnaird (25:39): Yeah, I think there was a couple of key things that I learned early on, and I'll give a shout out to Mark Harrison who owns an agency in Toronto called T one, and they also put on a sponsorship X or sponsorship forum called Sponsorship X. And I've known Mark for a number of years and he is also a member of our advisory board now, and I would almost say a mentor, but had been listening to some of the things he had been saying. And I think one of the most important things that I picked up from him was make sure you are telling your stakeholders, your sponsors, all the scenarios and work them through and have them involved in all of the scenarios that you're planning. And so that was one of the most critical things that I think we're able to do is have weekly or biweekly calls with all of our partners, all of our stakeholders, just to keep them up to date on what we were doing, what we were thinking. (26:52): So that was probably the critical thing, number one. Number two, we experimented a lot with different ideas. We said to our partners in particular with summer series that this is an opportunity to experiment with all sorts of new things. We created a, one example is we created a pre-show for our broadcast called Crankworx Connect where we would have athletes and ambassadors and brand people on to interview them, talk to them. And it was sort of like a 30 minute lead into our main broadcasts. We did all sorts of different things within the broadcasts. I mean, that was the other sort of crazy thing about the broadcast is everything was done remote. So we had our announcers like Camal and Elliot Jackson, I think Elliot was in his living room and Cam was in his garage in Oregon and California. We had our production team producing remotely in Quebec and our timing and data graphics team producing remotely in Germany. (28:07): And so we were trying all sorts of stuff to make this work. And I think we took some of that. It gave us an opportunity to experiment, but it gave our partners an opportunity to experiment as well. We took some of those learnings to Innsbrook. I mean we had to do that remotely as well. We did have a few more people on site because there was a lot of people based in Europe, so that made it slightly easier. But I think post that, the other thing that we did was we spent a lot of time doing what I've sort of coined a benefits audit or a deliverables audit. We went through the typical list of things that we provide for our partners at the varying levels of investment that they're involved with Crank Works and sort of thought, okay, which ones can we still do? (29:07): Which ones can we not do? And is there alternatives for the things that we can't do or is there new things we could do? And so when we had done that and sort of looked at, okay, here's the things we can offer, we started to have conversations with our partners about these things and made them aware and truly listened to what were the challenges and issues and problems that they were trying to solve, what keeps them up at night with work and tried to come up with new, whether it was broadcast or new digital solutions for those problems homes. And this has taken a truly whole of team approach to do that. And our team has adapted better than I think I've ever seen them adapt. I mean for a number of years have tried to get the, I mean every year we have a retreat and we sit down and we've developed our stakeholder wheel and trying to get the whole team to understand that yes, you might manage the athletes, but you also have an influence on sponsorship. (30:34): You also have an influence on the media or you also have an influence on the destinations that we go to. And so as we try to develop this whole of team approach, I kind of felt like we were maybe halfway there, but this forced us to get a hundred percent of the way there was for it forced people to get uncomfortable honestly, but to reexamine how they could support other things. And now we're in a much better position where we work hand in hand with our marketing and our digital social team, and they're so involved in our broadcast team, they're so involved now in all the, not directly in the conversations we have with partners, but we are bringing all this feedback from our partners to these teams and going, Hey everyone, we need to solve this stuff or deliver on these things as a very holistic approach. (31:38): And I think that was what we learned last summer. And from inberg last fall, we really spent a lot of time over this past winter developing that and spent a ton of time planning and preparing. And it's gotten to the point now where our social media schedule is, every single thing is planned to the minute and it's all color coded. Is this a sport delivery? Is this a destination delivery? Is it a sponsored delivery? What channels is it going in? Who at which brand or who are we sharing this with on the other end? Is it an athlete? Is it someone responsible for digital or marketing in a destination or ATRA or specialized or whoever? And so that's been critical to how we've been able to successfully deliver for partners in this new world.", "token_estimate": 1300, "speakers": ["Darren Kinnaird"], "start_timestamp": "25:39", "end_timestamp": "25:39", "podcast_index": 80, "episode_name": "Ep 80: Darren Kinnaird | Event Marketing, Maintaining Sponsorships and COVID | Crankworx", "slug": "ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "guest_name": "Darren Kinnaird", "guest_title": "Crankworx", "categories": [], "short_description": "Of all the industries to get hammered the most during COVID, the events industry has been hit hard. Forced to cancel, post-pone, shutter doors, and limit capacities. Events around the world have been struggling to operate with the restrictions.", "podcast_length": "52:50"}, {"id": "d222e2ae-d38c-4069-9f6f-251e639bd6c4", "episode_id": "8d72a24e-5891-43be-b320-ead7f102ad1d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4878", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Cole Heilborn (32:44): Wow. So what are some of the deliverables that you had to take off the table and what were some that you were able to adapt or add to the list for each of your partners?\n\nDarren Kinnaird (32:54): Yeah, I mean I think we've had to take away expo booths for obvious reasons, demo booths, sampling opportunities. I mean all the things that you would traditionally provide for partners at a festival we've lost for the most part, not fully, because I think in Innsbruck, they've actually come up with a really creative way of doing it, and I'll come back to it in a second, but a lot of those human to human connection, physical connection. But we think we've been able to come up with new ways to integrate branded content or brand messaging into our broadcasts or into our social really having, I mean, we've always tried to have this focus on everything we do delivers for all stakeholders as much as we possibly can, but we've kind of taken it to a whole other level in Innsbrook. They actually last fall and this spring they found a bit of a loophole. (34:06): So the event takes place up at this resort called Mutters, which is about a 20 minute drive out of downtown Innsbrook in Innsbruck. Farmer's markets were still allowed last fall and this spring. And so we held a farmer's market of bike expo in the city, and so it just needed to be fenced in, you needed to check in. There was a number of people that were allowed in and that was very successful, maybe more successful than the expo had ever been at the venue. And there's one thing that coming out of this, we will probably keep, I mean we went down a couple times into the city and the lineup to demo one of the new specialized e-bikes was 20 people deep all day long and just take a bike for an hour. And you're right in the city, but the mountains are two minutes away and you can be on the trails in a couple of minutes. So there's definitely been some great key learnings out of this.", "token_estimate": 494, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Darren Kinnaird"], "start_timestamp": "32:44", "end_timestamp": "32:54", "podcast_index": 80, "episode_name": "Ep 80: Darren Kinnaird | Event Marketing, Maintaining Sponsorships and COVID | Crankworx", "slug": "ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "guest_name": "Darren Kinnaird", "guest_title": "Crankworx", "categories": [], "short_description": "Of all the industries to get hammered the most during COVID, the events industry has been hit hard. Forced to cancel, post-pone, shutter doors, and limit capacities. Events around the world have been struggling to operate with the restrictions.", "podcast_length": "52:50"}, {"id": "3a88d6be-3fe4-4ad6-b693-7a48eeaf0f18", "episode_id": "8d72a24e-5891-43be-b320-ead7f102ad1d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4878", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (35:15): What strikes me as really interesting is I feel like all the news that we heard over the last year as events are responding to this stuff is like, well, got to close down. We don't know what else to do or we got to limit capacity or all the things that people have been trying or have been forced to do. I guess, and I guess I don't want to speak for other events, but what it sounds like you guys have really been able to do, and maybe this has been your mindset before the pandemic, but Crank Works is an event business. You are an event, but you're way more than just an event. And so that perspective shift maybe has allowed you guys to continue to operate and find new paths and avenues. The event isn't what makes Crank works well, it is and it isn't, but you see what I'm saying. Maybe?", "token_estimate": 204, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "35:15", "end_timestamp": "35:15", "podcast_index": 80, "episode_name": "Ep 80: Darren Kinnaird | Event Marketing, Maintaining Sponsorships and COVID | Crankworx", "slug": "ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "guest_name": "Darren Kinnaird", "guest_title": "Crankworx", "categories": [], "short_description": "Of all the industries to get hammered the most during COVID, the events industry has been hit hard. Forced to cancel, post-pone, shutter doors, and limit capacities. Events around the world have been struggling to operate with the restrictions.", "podcast_length": "52:50"}, {"id": "be8455c0-d76e-4312-bd12-509a3abf8f6c", "episode_id": "8d72a24e-5891-43be-b320-ead7f102ad1d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4878", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Darren Kinnaird (36:04): Yeah, I mean, I think one of the things that struck me prior to the pandemic is, and I mean I sometimes live within the bubble of crank work, so I don't quite have always the perspective that someone on the outside would have. But internally, what has started to happen over the last few years leading into 2019 was this concept of the Crankworx family and that involved the different destinations we went to and the people that we worked with there, but also the athletes. I mean, I can remember in 2018 we did the first ever Crank Horse World Tour awards and we had a new award, the Crankworx Spirit Award, and Martin Soderstrom won the first year and the guy was almost in tears on the stage when he won the award because for him, it showed that he was embodying and living this spirit of what it meant to be part of the Crank Works family. (37:25): Anna Cab Beton won it the next year and she still talks about it as one of the most important awards she's ever won. And so I think this Crankworx family idea goes beyond just the athletes and the partners and the destinations and so many people just they identify with the concept of I love mountain biking, I love celebrating mountain biking, and that's what Crankworx does. And so even though they can't attend, they can still be a part of the action, whether it's social, whether it's through our watching or broadcast. And that actually just sparks a thought here. I remember something, one of the things we did realize in Innsbruck this spring was that people wanted to see these behind the scenes moments. There was a way for us to share these sort of non-broadcast non-sport moments to a wider audience. And so it was little things like the Scrub Off, which was an event that we literally made up the morning of Slopestyle as a alternative. (38:48): If the weather came in and we needed to kill some time, well we'll do a scrub off. And that was one of the best events of Crank sbr and all these photos and images. And we were able to share that even though people couldn't be there and tens of thousands of people watched this content of this scrub off about something that was just made up that morning. We've taken that into Crank Works BC to have a videographer just running around behind the scenes, capturing goofy moments that the athletes are having and that desire to be a part of this cultural movement that is crankworx that I think is carrying or helping carry us right now. I mean, if you go back to the roots of what Crankworx was about, and I think Rob talked about this when he was on nowhere in the world could you be rubbing shoulders in the lift thigh with the biggest mountain bike superstars in the world or riding in the same race as them. I mean, that was something we started way back in 2004 and say we, but Crankworx started back in 2004 as a goal is to bring all these people together to celebrate mountain biking. And that is still such an important part of what we do today and will be a massive part of what we do in the future.", "token_estimate": 761, "speakers": ["Darren Kinnaird"], "start_timestamp": "36:04", "end_timestamp": "36:04", "podcast_index": 80, "episode_name": "Ep 80: Darren Kinnaird | Event Marketing, Maintaining Sponsorships and COVID | Crankworx", "slug": "ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "guest_name": "Darren Kinnaird", "guest_title": "Crankworx", "categories": [], "short_description": "Of all the industries to get hammered the most during COVID, the events industry has been hit hard. Forced to cancel, post-pone, shutter doors, and limit capacities. Events around the world have been struggling to operate with the restrictions.", "podcast_length": "52:50"}, {"id": "ef7a1632-c18e-46d7-ba07-121a4b9579ad", "episode_id": "8d72a24e-5891-43be-b320-ead7f102ad1d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4878", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Cole Heilborn (40:17): So as marketers, brands are trying to figure out how does event marketing fit into our plans for next year? We're recording this here middle of October. A lot of brands are planning for 2022, et cetera. I guess what would you say to them as they're trying to consider, are these events still viable? How can we get value out of them?\n\nDarren Kinnaird (40:40): Are you saying that from the sponsor perspective or from the event organizer perspective?\n\nCole Heilborn (40:45): If you were talking to a spons and they're like, we're just not sure is this valuable to us, it's not going to be the same. Or if it's not the same, how are we still going to get the same return?", "token_estimate": 173, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Darren Kinnaird"], "start_timestamp": "40:17", "end_timestamp": "40:45", "podcast_index": 80, "episode_name": "Ep 80: Darren Kinnaird | Event Marketing, Maintaining Sponsorships and COVID | Crankworx", "slug": "ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-80-darren-kinnaird-event-marketing-maintaining-sponsorships-and-covid-crankworx", "guest_name": "Darren Kinnaird", "guest_title": "Crankworx", "categories": [], "short_description": "Of all the industries to get hammered the most during COVID, the events industry has been hit hard. Forced to cancel, post-pone, shutter doors, and limit capacities. Events around the world have been struggling to operate with the restrictions.", "podcast_length": "52:50"}]
